#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:2
msgid "Code style guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:6
msgid ""
"When contributing to Rebel Engine's source code, you will be expected to "
"follow the style guidelines outlined below. Some of them are checked via the "
"Continuous Integration process and reviewers will ask you to fix potential "
"issues, so best setup your system as outlined below to ensure all your "
"commits follow the guidelines."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:13
msgid "C++ and Objective-C"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:15
msgid ""
"There are no written guidelines, but the code style agreed upon by the "
"developers is enforced via the `clang-format <https://clang.llvm.org/docs/"
"ClangFormat.html>`__ code beautifier, which takes care for you of all our "
"conventions. To name a few:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:20
msgid ""
"Indentation and alignment are both tab based (respectively one and two tabs)"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:21
msgid "One space around math and assignments operators as well as after commas"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:22
msgid ""
"Pointer and reference operators are affixed to the variable identifier, not "
"to the type name"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:24
msgid "See further down regarding header includes"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:26
msgid ""
"The rules used by clang-format are outlined in the `.clang-format <https://"
"github.com/RebelToolbox/RebelEngine/blob/main/.clang-format>`__ file of the "
"Rebel Engine repository."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:30
msgid ""
"As long as you ensure that your style matches the surrounding code and that "
"you not introducing trailing whitespace or space-based indentation, you "
"should be fine. If you plan to contribute regularly however, we strongly "
"advise that you setup clang-format locally to check and automatically fix "
"all your commits."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:35
msgid ""
"Rebel's code style should *not* be applied to third-party code, i.e. that is "
"included in Rebel Engine's source tree but was not written specifically for "
"our project. Such code usually come from different upstream projects with "
"their own style guides (or lack thereof), and don't want to introduce "
"differences that would make syncing with upstream repositories harder."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:42
msgid ""
"Third-party code is usually included in the ``thirdparty/`` folder and can "
"thus easily be excluded from formatting scripts. For the rare cases where a "
"third-party code snippet needs to be included directly within a Rebel Engine "
"file, you can use ``/* clang-format off */`` and ``/* clang-format on */`` "
"to tell clang-format to ignore a chunk of code."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:51
msgid ""
"These guidelines only cover code formatting. See :doc:`cpp_usage_guidelines` "
"for a list of language features that are permitted in pull requests."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:55
msgid "Using clang-format locally"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:57
msgid "First of all, you will need to install clang-format."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:60
msgid "Installing clang-format"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:62
msgid "Here's how to install clang-format:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:64
msgid ""
"Linux: It will usually be available out-of-the-box with the clang toolchain "
"packaged by your distribution. If your distro version is not the required "
"one, you can download a pre-compiled version from the `LLVM website <https://"
"releases.llvm.org/download.html>`__, or if you are on a Debian derivative, "
"use the `upstream repos <https://apt.llvm.org/>`__."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:69
msgid ""
"macOS and Windows: You can download precompiled binaries from the `LLVM "
"website <https://releases.llvm.org/download.html>`__. You may need to add "
"the path to the binary's folder to your system's ``PATH`` environment "
"variable to be able to call ``clang-format`` out of the box."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:74
msgid ""
"You then have different possibilities to apply clang-format to your changes:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:77
msgid "Running clang-format"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:79
msgid ""
"You can apply clang-format manually one or more files with the following "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:86
msgid ""
"``-i`` means that the changes should be written directly to the file (by "
"default clang-format would only output the fixed version to the terminal)."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:88
msgid ""
"The path can point to several files, either one after the other or using "
"wildcards like in a typical Unix shell. Be careful when globbing so that you "
"don't run clang-format on compiled objects (.o and .a files) that are in "
"Rebel Engine's tree. So better use ``core/*.{cpp,h}`` than ``core/*``."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:94
msgid "Pre-commit clang-format hook"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:96
msgid ""
"For ease of use, we provide a pre-commit hook for Git that will run clang-"
"format automatically on all your commits to check them, and let you apply "
"its changes in the final commit."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:100
msgid ""
"This \"hook\" is a script which can be found in ``tools/hooks``, refer to "
"that folder's README.md for installation instructions."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:103
msgid ""
"If your clang-format is not in the ``PATH``, you may have to edit the ``pre-"
"commit-clang-format`` to point to the correct binary for it to work. The "
"hook was tested on Linux and macOS, but should also work in the Git Shell on "
"Windows."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:109
msgid "IDE plugin"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:111
msgid ""
"Most IDEs or code editors have beautifier plugins that can be configured to "
"run clang-format automatically, for example each time you save a file."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:114
msgid "Here is a non-exhaustive list of beautifier plugins for some IDEs:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:116
msgid ""
"Qt Creator: `Beautifier plugin <https://doc.qt.io/qtcreator/creator-"
"beautifier.html>`__"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:117
msgid ""
"Visual Studio Code: `Clang-Format <https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/"
"items?itemName=xaver.clang-format>`__"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:118
msgid ""
"Visual Studio: `ClangFormat <https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?"
"itemName=LLVMExtensions.ClangFormat>`__"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:119
msgid "vim: `vim-clang-format <https://github.com/rhysd/vim-clang-format>`__"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:120
msgid ""
"CLion: Starting from version ``2019.1``, no plugin is required. Instead, "
"enable `ClangFormat <https://www.jetbrains.com/help/clion/clangformat-as-"
"alternative-formatter.html#clion-support>`__"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:123
msgid "(Pull requests welcome to extend this list with tested plugins.)"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:126
msgid "Header includes"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:128
msgid ""
"When adding new C++ or Objective-C files or including new headers in "
"existing ones, the following rules should be followed:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:131
msgid ""
"The first lines in the file should be Rebel Engine's copyright header and "
"MIT license, copy-pasted from another file. Make sure to adjust the filename."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:133
msgid ""
"In a ``.h`` header, include guards should be used with the form "
"``FILENAME_H``."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:136
msgid ""
"In a ``.cpp`` file (e.g. ``filename.cpp``), the first include should be the "
"one where the class is declared (e.g. ``#include \"filename.h\"``), followed "
"by an empty line for separation."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:139
msgid ""
"Then come headers from Rebel Engine's own code base, included in "
"alphabetical order (enforced by ``clang-format``) with paths relative to the "
"root folder. Those includes should be done with quotes, e.g. ``#include "
"\"core/object.h\"``. The block of Rebel Engine header includes should then "
"be followed by an empty line for separation."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:144
msgid ""
"Finally, third-party headers (either from ``thirdparty`` or from the "
"system's include paths) come next and should be included with the < and > "
"symbols, e.g. ``#include <png.h>``. The block of third-party headers should "
"also be followed by an empty line for separation."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/code_style_guidelines.rst:148
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine and third-party headers should be included in the file that "
"requires them, i.e. in the `.h` header if used in the declarative code or in "
"the `.cpp` if used only in the imperative code."
msgstr ""

