#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:2
msgid "C++ usage guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:5
#: development/compiling/optimizing_for_size.rst:7
msgid "Rationale"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:7
msgid ""
"The C++ standard used throughout the codebase is a subset of **C++17**. "
"While modern C++ brings a lot of opportunities to write faster, more "
"readable code, we chose to restrict our usage of C++ to a subset for a few "
"reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:12
msgid ""
"It makes it easier to review code in online editors. This is because engine "
"contributors don't always have access to a full-featured IDE while reviewing "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:15
msgid ""
"It makes the code easier to grasp for beginner contributors (who may not be "
"professional C++ programmers). Rebel Engine's codebase is known to be easy "
"to learn from, and we'd like to keep it that way."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:19
msgid ""
"To get your pull request merged, it needs to follow the C++ usage guidelines "
"outlined here. Of course, you can use features not allowed here in your own "
"C++ modules or GDNative scripts."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:25
msgid ""
"The guidelines below don't apply to third-party dependencies, although we "
"generally favor small libraries instead of larger solutions. See also :doc:"
"`best_practices_for_engine_contributors`."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:31
msgid "See :doc:`code_style_guidelines` for formatting guidelines."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:34
msgid "Disallowed features"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:36
msgid ""
"**Any feature not listed below is allowed.** Using features like "
"``constexpr`` variables and ``nullptr`` is encouraged when possible. Still, "
"try to keep your use of modern C++ features conservative. Their use needs to "
"serve a real purpose, such as improving code readability or performance."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:42
msgid "Standard Template Library"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:44
msgid ""
"We don't allow using the `STL <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Standard_Template_Library>`__ as Rebel Engine provides its own data types "
"(among other things). See :ref:`about/faq:why does rebel engine not use stl "
"(standard template library)?` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:48
msgid ""
"This means that pull requests should **not** use ``std::string``, ``std::"
"vector`` and the like. Instead, use Rebel Engine's datatypes as described "
"below:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:51
msgid "Use ``String`` instead of ``std::string``."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:52
msgid ""
"Use ``Vector`` instead of ``std::vector``. In some cases, ``LocalVector`` "
"can be used as an alternative (ask core developers first)."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:54
msgid "Use ``Array`` instead of ``std::array``."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:58
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine also has a List datatype (which is a linked list). While List "
"is already used in the codebase, it typically performs worse than other "
"datatypes like Vector and Array. Therefore, List should be avoided in new "
"code unless necessary."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:63
msgid "``auto`` keyword"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:65
msgid ""
"Please don't use the ``auto`` keyword for type inference. While it can avoid "
"repetition, it can also lead to confusing code:"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:76
msgid ""
"Keep in mind hover documentation often isn't readily available for pull "
"request reviewers. Most of the time, reviewers will use GitHub's online "
"viewer to review pull requests."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:80
msgid ""
"We chose to forbid ``auto`` instead of allowing it on a case-by-case basis "
"to avoid having to decide on difficult edge cases. Thank you for your "
"understanding."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:84
msgid "Lambdas"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:86
msgid ""
"Lambdas should be used conservatively when they make code effectively faster "
"or simpler, and do not impede readability. Please ask before using lambdas "
"in a pull request."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:91
msgid "``#pragma once`` directive"
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:93
msgid ""
"To follow the existing style, please use standard ``#ifdef``-based include "
"guards instead of ``#pragma once`` in new files."
msgstr ""

#: contributing/cpp_usage_guidelines.rst:98
msgid ""
"See :ref:`contributing/code_style_guidelines:header includes` for guidelines "
"on sorting includes in C++ and Objective-C files."
msgstr ""

