#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:2
#, fuzzy
msgid "Introduction to Rebel"
msgstr "تعارف"

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:4
msgid ""
"This article is here to help you figure out whether Rebel might be a good "
"fit for you. We will introduce some broad features of the engine to give you "
"a feel for what you can achieve with it and answer questions such as \"what "
"do I need to know to get started?\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:9
msgid ""
"This is by no means an exhaustive overview. We will introduce many more "
"features in this getting started series."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:13
msgid "What is Rebel?"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:15
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine is a general-purpose 2D and 3D game engine designed to support "
"all sorts of projects. You can use it to create games or applications you "
"can then release on desktop or mobile, as well as on the web."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:19
msgid ""
"You can also create console games with it, although you either need strong "
"programming skills or a developer to port the game for you."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:22
msgid ""
"The Rebel Toolbox team can't provide an open-source console export due to "
"the licensing terms imposed by console manufacturers. Regardless of the "
"engine you use, though, releasing games on consoles is always a lot of work. "
"You can read more on that here: :doc:`/tutorials/platforms/consoles`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:28
msgid "How does it work and look?"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:30
msgid ""
"Rebel comes with a fully-fledged Rebel Editor with integrated tools to "
"answer the most common needs. It includes a code editor, an animation "
"editor, a tilemap editor, a shader editor, a debugger, a profiler, and more."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:36
msgid ""
"The team strives to offer a feature-rich game editor with a consistent user "
"experience. While there is always room for improvement, the user interface "
"keeps getting refined."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:40
msgid ""
"Of course, if you prefer, you can work with external programs. We officially "
"support importing 3D scenes designed in Blender_. We also support Visual "
"Studio for C# on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:47
#: tutorials/scripting/index.rst:12
msgid "Programming languages"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:49
msgid "Let's talk about the available programming languages."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:51
msgid ""
"You can code your games using :ref:`GDScript <toc-learn-scripting-"
"gdscript>`, a Rebel-specific and tightly integrated language with a "
"lightweight syntax, or :ref:`C# <toc-learn-scripting-C#>`, which is popular "
"in the games industry. These are the two main scripting languages we support."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:56
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine also supports a node-based visual programming language named :"
"ref:`VisualScript <toc-learn-scripting-visual_script>`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:59
msgid ""
"With the :ref:`GDNative <toc-tutorials-gdnative>` technology, you can also "
"write gameplay or high-performance algorithms in C or C++ without "
"recompiling the engine. You can use this technology to integrate third-party "
"libraries and other Software Development Kits (SDK) in the engine."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:64
msgid ""
"Of course, you can also directly add modules and features to the engine, as "
"it's completely free and open-source."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:67
msgid ""
"These are the five officially supported programming languages. The community "
"maintains support for many more. For more information, see :ref:`GDNative "
"third-party bindings <tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative:version "
"compatibility>`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:73
msgid "What do I need to know to use Rebel Engine?"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:75
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine is a feature-packed game engine. With its thousands of "
"features, there is a lot to learn. To make the most of it, you need good "
"programming foundations. While we try to make the engine accessible, you "
"will benefit a lot from knowing how to think like a programmer first."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:80
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine relies on the object-oriented programming paradigm. Being "
"comfortable with concepts such as classes and objects will help you code "
"efficiently in it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:83
msgid ""
"If you are entirely new to programming, we recommend following the `CS50 "
"open courseware`_ from Harvard University. It's a great free course that "
"will teach you everything you need to know to be off to a good start. It "
"will save you countless hours and hurdles learning any game engine afterward."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:88
msgid ""
"In CS50, you will learn multiple programming languages. Don't be afraid of "
"that: programming languages have many similarities. The skills you learn "
"with one language transfer well to others."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:92
msgid ""
"We will provide you with more Rebel-specific learning resources in :doc:"
"`learning_new_features`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/introduction_to_rebel.rst:95
msgid ""
"In the next part, you will get an overview of the engine's essential "
"concepts."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:6
msgid "Overview of Rebel Engine's key concepts"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:8
msgid ""
"Every game engine revolves around abstractions you use to build your "
"applications. In Rebel Engine, a game is a **tree** of **nodes** that you "
"group together into **scenes**. You can then wire these nodes so they can "
"communicate using **signals**."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:13
msgid ""
"These are the four concepts you will learn here. We're going to look at them "
"briefly to give you a sense of how the engine works. In the getting started "
"series, you will get to use them in practice."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:18
#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:36
#: tutorials/best_practices/applying_object_oriented_principles.rst:47
msgid "Scenes"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:20
msgid ""
"In the Rebel Editor, you break down your game in reusable scenes. A scene "
"can be a character, a weapon, a menu in the user interface, a single house, "
"an entire level, or anything you can think of. Rebel scenes are flexible; "
"they fill the role of both prefabs and scenes in some other game engines."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:27
msgid ""
"You can also nest scenes. For example, you can put your character in a "
"level, and drag and drop a scene as a child of it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:35
msgid ""
"A scene is composed of one or more **nodes**. Nodes are your game's smallest "
"building blocks that you arrange into trees. Here's an example of a "
"character's nodes."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:41
msgid ""
"It is made of a ``KinematicBody2D`` node named \"Character\", a ``Sprite``, "
"a ``Camera2D``, and a ``CollisionShape2D``."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:44
msgid ""
"The node names end with \"2D\" because this is a 2D scene. Their 3D "
"counterpart have names that end with \"3D\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:47
msgid ""
"Notice how nodes and scenes look the same in the editor. When you save a "
"tree of nodes as a scene, it then shows as a single node, with its internal "
"structure hidden in the editor."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:51
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine provides an extensive library of base node types you can "
"combine and extend to build more powerful ones. 2D, 3D, or user interface, "
"you will do most things with these nodes."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:60
msgid ""
"All your game's scenes come together in the **scene tree**, literally a tree "
"of scenes. And as scenes are trees of nodes, the scene tree also is a tree "
"of nodes. But it's easier to think of your game in terms of scenes as they "
"can represent characters, weapons, doors, or your user interface."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:70
msgid ""
"Nodes emit signals when some event occurs. This feature allows you to make "
"nodes communicate without hard-wiring them in code. It gives you a lot of "
"flexibility in how you structure your scenes."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:76
msgid ""
"Signals are Rebel's version of the *observer* pattern. You can read more "
"about it here: https://gameprogrammingpatterns.com/observer.html"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:80
msgid ""
"For example, buttons emit a signal when pressed. You can connect to this "
"signal to run code in reaction to this event, like starting the game or "
"opening a menu."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:83
msgid ""
"Other built-in signals can tell you when two objects collided, when a "
"character or monster entered a given area, and much more. You can also "
"define new signals tailored to your game."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:88
#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:210
#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:165
#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:178
#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:373
#: tutorials/2d/2d_movement.rst:351 tutorials/2d/2d_sprite_animation.rst:261
#: tutorials/export/exporting_pcks.rst:136
#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:372
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:90
msgid ""
"Nodes, scenes, the scene tree, and signals are four core concepts in Rebel "
"Engine that you will manipulate all the time."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:93
msgid ""
"Nodes are your game's smallest building blocks. You combine them to create "
"scenes that you then combine and nest into the scene tree. You can then use "
"signals to make nodes react to events in other nodes or different scene tree "
"branches."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview.rst:97
msgid ""
"After this short breakdown, you probably have many questions. Bear with us "
"as you will get many answers throughout the getting started series."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:6
msgid "Learning new features"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:8
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine is a feature-rich game engine. There is a lot to learn about "
"it. This page explains how you can use the online manual, built-in code "
"reference, and join online communities to learn new features and techniques."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:13
msgid "Making the most of this manual"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:15
msgid ""
"What you are reading now is the user manual. It documents each of the "
"engine's concepts and available features. When learning a new topic, you can "
"start by browsing the corresponding section of this website. The left menu "
"allows you to explore broad topics while the search bar will help you find "
"more specific pages. If a page exists for a given theme, it will often link "
"to more related content."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:24
msgid ""
"The manual has a companion API documentation that explains each Rebel Engine "
"class's available functions and properties when programming. While the "
"manual covers general features, concepts, and how to use the editor, the "
"reference is all about using Rebel Engine's scripting API (Application "
"Programming Interface). You can access it both online and offline. We "
"recommend browsing the reference offline, from within the Rebel Editor. To "
"do so, go to Help -> Search or press :kbd:`F1`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:34
msgid ""
"To browse it online, head to the manual's :ref:`Rebel Engine API <toc-api>` "
"section."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:37
msgid "A class's API documentation page tells you:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:39
msgid ""
"Where the class exists in the inheritance hierarchy. You can click the top "
"links to jump to parent classes and see the properties and methods a type "
"inherits."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:45
msgid "A summary of the class's role and use cases."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:47
msgid ""
"An explanation of the class's properties, methods, signals, enums, and "
"constants."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:50
msgid "Links to manual pages further detailing the class."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:52
msgid ""
"If the manual or API documentation is missing or has insufficient "
"information, please open an Issue in the official `Rebel Documentation "
"<https://github.com/RebelToolbox/RebelDocumentation>`__ GitHub repository to "
"report it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:57
msgid ""
"You can Ctrl-click any underlined text like the name of a class, property, "
"method, signal, or constant to jump to it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:61
msgid "Learning to think like a programmer"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:63
msgid ""
"Teaching programming foundations and how to think like a game developer is "
"beyond the scope of the Rebel Documentation. If you're new to programming, "
"we recommend two excellent free resources to get you started:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:67
msgid ""
"Harvard university offers a free courseware to learn to program, `CS50 "
"<https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/>`__. It will teach you programming "
"fundamentals, how code works, and how to think like a programmer. These "
"skills are essential to become a game developer and learn any game engine "
"efficiently. You can see this course as an investment that will save you "
"time and trouble when you learn to create games."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:74
msgid ""
"If you prefer books, check out the free ebook `Automate The Boring Stuff "
"With Python <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/>`__ by Al Sweigart."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:78
msgid "Learning with the community"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:80
msgid "Rebel Toolbox has a growing community of users."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:82
msgid ""
"Asking questions well and providing details will help others answer you "
"faster and better. When asking questions, we recommend including the "
"following information:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:86
msgid ""
"**Describe your goal**. You want to explain what you are trying to achieve "
"design-wise. If you are having trouble figuring out how to make a solution "
"work, there may be a different, easier solution that accomplishes the same "
"goal."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:91
msgid ""
"If there is an error involved, **share the exact error message**. You can "
"copy the exact error message in the editor's Debugger bottom panel by "
"clicking the Copy Error icon. Knowing what it says can help community "
"members better identify how you triggered the error."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:96
msgid ""
"If there is code involved, **share a code sample**. Other users won't be "
"able to help you fix a problem without seeing your code. Share the code as "
"text directly. To do so, you can copy and paste a short code snippet in a "
"chat box, or use a website like `Pastebin <https://pastebin.com/>`__ to "
"share long files."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:102
msgid ""
"**Share a screenshot** of your *Scene* dock along with your written code. "
"Most of the code you write affects nodes in your scenes. As a result, you "
"should think of those scenes as part of your source code."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:108
msgid ""
"Also, please don't take a picture with your phone, the low quality and "
"screen reflections can make it hard to understand the image. Your operating "
"system should have a built-in tool to take screenshots with the :kbd:`PrtSc` "
"(Print Screen) key."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:113
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can use a program like `ShareX <https://getsharex.com/"
">`__ on Windows or `FlameShot <https://flameshot.org/>`__ on Linux."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:116
msgid ""
"Sharing a video of your running game can also be really **useful to "
"troubleshoot your game**. You can use programs like `OBS Studio <https://"
"obsproject.com/>`__ and `Screen to GIF <https://www.screentogif.com/>`__ to "
"capture your screen."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:121
msgid ""
"You can then use a service like `streamable <https://streamable.com/>`__ or "
"a cloud provider to upload and share your videos for free."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:124
msgid ""
"If you're not using the stable version of Rebel Engine, please mention the "
"version you're using. The answer can be different as available features and "
"the interface evolve rapidly."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/introduction/learning_new_features.rst:128
msgid ""
"Following these guidelines will maximize your chances of getting the answer "
"you're looking for. They will save time both to you and the persons helping "
"you."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/index.rst:4
msgid ""
"This series builds upon the :ref:`Introduction to Rebel <toc-learn-"
"introduction>` and will get you started with the editor and the engine. You "
"will learn more about nodes and scenes, code your first classes with "
"GDScript, use signals to make nodes communicate with one another, and more."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/index.rst:9
msgid ""
"The following lessons are here to prepare you for :doc:`/getting_started/"
"first_2d_game/index`, a step-by-step tutorial where you will code a game "
"from scratch. By the end of it, you will have the necessary foundations to "
"explore more features in other sections. We also included links to pages "
"that cover a given topic in-depth where appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:2
msgid "Creating instances"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:4
msgid ""
"In the previous part, we saw that a scene is a collection of nodes organized "
"in a tree structure, with a single node as its root. You can split your "
"project into any number of scenes. This feature helps you break down and "
"organize your game's different components."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:9
msgid ""
"You can create as many scenes as you'd like and save them as files with the "
"``.tscn`` extension, which stands for \"text scene\". The ``Label.tscn`` "
"file from the previous lesson was an example. We call those files \"Packed "
"Scenes\" as they pack information about your scene's content."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:14
msgid ""
"Here's an example of a ball. It's composed of a :ref:`RigidBody2D "
"<class_RigidBody2D>` node as its root named Ball, which allows the ball to "
"fall and bounce on walls, a :ref:`Sprite <class_Sprite>` node, and a :ref:"
"`CollisionShape2D <class_CollisionShape2D>`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:21
msgid ""
"Once you saved a scene, it works as a blueprint: you can reproduce it in "
"other scenes as many times as you'd like. Replicating an object from a "
"template like this is called **instancing**."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:27
msgid ""
"As we mentioned in the previous part, instanced scenes behave like a node: "
"the editor hides their content by default. When you instance the Ball, you "
"only see the Ball node. Notice also how each duplicate has a unique name."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:31
msgid ""
"Every instance of the Ball scene starts with the same structure and "
"properties as ``Ball.tscn``. However, you can modify each independently, "
"such as changing how they bounce, how heavy they are, or any property "
"exposed by the source scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:37
msgid "In practice"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:39
msgid ""
"Let's use instancing in practice to see how it works in Rebel Engine. We "
"invite you to download the ball's sample project we prepared for you: :"
"download:`instancing.zip <files/instancing.zip>`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:43
msgid ""
"Extract the archive on your computer. Then, open the Rebel Editor, and in "
"the project manager, click the Import button to import the project."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:48
msgid ""
"In the pop-up that appears, click the browse button and navigate to the "
"folder you extracted."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:53
msgid "Double-click the ``project.rebel`` file to open it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:57
msgid "Finally, click the Import & Edit button."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:61
msgid ""
"The project contains two packed scenes: ``Main.tscn``, containing walls "
"against which the ball collides, and ``Ball.tscn``. The Main scene should "
"open automatically."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:67
msgid ""
"Let's add a ball as a child of the Main node. In the Scene dock, select the "
"Main node. Then, click the link icon at the top of the scene dock. This "
"button allows you to add an instance of a scene as a child of the currently "
"selected node."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:73
msgid "Double-click the ball scene to instance it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:77
msgid "The ball appears in the top-left corner of the viewport."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:81
msgid "Click on it and drag it towards the center of the view."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:85
msgid "Play the game by pressing F5. You should see it fall."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:87
msgid ""
"Now, we want to create more instances of the Ball node. With the ball still "
"selected, press :kbd:`Ctrl-D` (:kbd:`Cmd-D` on macOS) to call the duplicate "
"command. Click and drag to move the new ball to a different location."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:93
msgid "You can repeat this process until you have several in the scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:97
msgid ""
"Play the game again. You should now see every ball fall independently from "
"one another. This is what instances do. Each is an independent reproduction "
"of a template scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:102
msgid "Editing scenes and instances"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:104
msgid "There is more to instances. With this feature, you can:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:106
msgid ""
"Change the properties of one ball without affecting the others using the "
"Inspector."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:108
msgid ""
"Change the default properties of every Ball by opening the ``Ball.tscn`` "
"scene and making a change to the Ball node there. Upon saving, all instances "
"of the Ball in the project will see their values update."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:112
msgid ""
"Changing a property on an instance always overrides values from the "
"corresponding packed scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:115
msgid ""
"Let's try this. Open ``Ball.tscn`` and select the Ball node. In the "
"Inspector on the right, click on the PhysicsMaterial property to expand it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:120
msgid ""
"Set its Bounce property to ``2`` by clicking on the number field, typing "
"``2``, and pressing :kbd:`Enter`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:125
msgid ""
"Play the game by pressing :kbd:`F5` and notice how all balls now bounce a "
"lot more. As the Ball scene is a template for all instances, modifying it "
"and saving causes all instances to update accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:129
msgid ""
"Let's now adjust an individual instance. Head back to the Main scene by "
"clicking on the corresponding tab above the viewport."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:134
msgid ""
"Select one of the instanced Ball nodes and, in the Inspector, set its "
"Gravity Scale value to ``10``."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:139
msgid "A grey \"revert\" button appears next to the adjusted property."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:143
msgid ""
"This icon indicates you are overriding a value from the source packed scene. "
"Even if you modify the property in the original scene, the value override "
"will be preserved in the instance. Clicking the revert icon will restore the "
"property to the value in the saved scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:148
msgid ""
"Rerun the game and notice how this ball now falls much faster than the "
"others."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:150
msgid ""
"If you change a value on the ``PhysicsMaterial`` of one instance, it will "
"affect all the others. This is because ``PhysicsMaterial`` is a resource, "
"and resources are shared between instances. To make a resource unique for "
"one instance, right-click on it in the Inspector and click Make Unique in "
"the contextual menu."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:156
msgid ""
"Resources are another essential building block of Rebel games we will cover "
"in a later lesson."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:160
msgid "Scene instances as a design language"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:162
msgid ""
"Instances and scenes in Rebel Engine offer an excellent design language, "
"setting the engine apart from others out there. We designed Rebel Engine "
"around this concept from the ground up."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:166
msgid ""
"We recommend dismissing architectural code patterns when making games with "
"the Rebel Editor, such as Model-View-Controller (MVC) or Entity-Relationship "
"diagrams. Instead, you can start by imagining the elements players will see "
"in your game and structure your code around them."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:171
msgid "For example, you could break down a shooter game like so:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:175
msgid ""
"You can come up with a diagram like this for almost any type of game. Each "
"rectangle represents an entity that's visible in the game from the player's "
"perspective. The arrows tell you which scene owns which."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:179
msgid ""
"Once you have a diagram, we recommended creating a scene for each element "
"listed in it to develop your game. You'll use instancing, either by code or "
"directly in the editor, to build your tree of scenes."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:183
msgid ""
"Programmers tend to spend a lot of time designing abstract architectures and "
"trying to fit components into it. Designing based on scenes makes "
"development faster and more straightforward, allowing you to focus on the "
"game logic itself. Because most game components map directly to a scene, "
"using a design based on scene instantiation means you need little other "
"architectural code."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:189
msgid ""
"Here's the example of a scene diagram for an open-world game with tons of "
"assets and nested elements:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:194
msgid ""
"Imagine we started by creating the room. We could make a couple of different "
"room scenes, with unique arrangements of furniture in them. Later, we could "
"make a house scene that uses multiple room instances for the interior. We "
"would create a citadel out of many instanced houses and a large terrain on "
"which we would place the citadel. Each of these would be a scene instancing "
"one or more sub-scenes."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:200
msgid ""
"Later, we could create scenes representing guards and add them to the "
"citadel. They would be indirectly added to the overall game world."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:203
msgid ""
"With the Rebel Editor, it's easy to iterate on your game like this, as all "
"you need to do is create and instantiate more scenes. We designed the editor "
"to be accessible to programmers, designers, and artists alike. A typical "
"team development process can involve 2D or 3D artists, level designers, game "
"designers, and animators, all working with the Rebel Editor."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:212
msgid ""
"Instancing, the process of producing an object from a blueprint has many "
"handy uses. With scenes, it gives you:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:215
msgid "The ability to divide your game into reusable components."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:216
msgid "A tool to structure and encapsulate complex systems."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/instancing.rst:217
msgid ""
"A language to think about your game project's structure in a natural way."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:4
msgid "Nodes and Scenes"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:6
msgid ""
"In :doc:`/getting_started/introduction/key_concepts_overview`, we saw that a "
"Rebel game is a tree of scenes and that each scene is a tree of nodes. In "
"this lesson, we explain a bit more about them. You will also create your "
"first scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:13
msgid ""
"**Nodes are the fundamental building blocks of your game**. They are like "
"the ingredients in a recipe. There are dozens of kinds that can display an "
"image, play a sound, represent a camera, and much more."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:19
msgid "All nodes have the following attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:21
msgid "A name."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:22
msgid "Editable properties."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:23
msgid "They receive callbacks to update every frame."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:24
msgid "You can extend them with new properties and functions."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:25
msgid "You can add them to another node as a child."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:27
msgid ""
"The last attribute is key. **Together, nodes form a tree**, which is a "
"powerful feature to organize projects. Since different nodes have different "
"functions, combining them produces more complex behavior. As we saw before, "
"you can build a playable character the camera follows using a kinematic body "
"node named \"Character\", a sprite node, a camera node, and a collision "
"shape node."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:38
msgid ""
"When you organize nodes in a tree, like our character, we call this "
"construct a scene. Once saved, scenes work like new node types in the "
"editor, where you can add them as a child of an existing node. In that case, "
"the instance of the scene appears as a single node with its internals hidden."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:43
msgid ""
"Scenes allow you to structure your game's code however you want. You can "
"**compose nodes** to create custom and complex node types, like a game "
"character that runs and jumps, a life bar, a chest with which you can "
"interact, and more."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:49
msgid ""
"The Rebel Editor essentially is a **scene editor**. It has plenty of tools "
"for editing 2D and 3D scenes, as well as user interfaces. A Rebel project "
"can contain as many of these scenes as you need. The engine only requires "
"one as your application's **main scene**. This is the scene Rebel Engine "
"will first load when you or a player runs the game."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:55
msgid "On top of acting like nodes, scenes have the following attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:57
msgid "They always have one root node, like the \"Character\" in our example."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:58
msgid "You can save them to your hard drive and load them later."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:59
msgid ""
"You can create as many instances of a scene as you'd like. You could have "
"five or ten characters in your game, created from your Character scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:63
msgid "Creating your first scene"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:65
msgid ""
"Let's create our first scene with a single node. To do so, you will need to "
"create a new project first. After opening the project, you should see an "
"empty editor."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:71
msgid ""
"In an empty scene, the Scene dock on the left shows several options to add a "
"root node quickly. \"2D Scene\" adds a Node2D node, \"3D Scene\" adds a "
"Spatial node, and \"User Interface\" adds a Control node. These presets are "
"here for convenience; they are not mandatory. \"Other Node\" lets you select "
"any node to be the root node. In an empty scene, \"Other Node\" is "
"equivalent to pressing the \"Add Child Node\" button at the top-left of the "
"Scene dock, which usually adds a new node as a child of the currently "
"selected node."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:79
msgid ""
"We're going to add a single Label node to our scene. Its function is to draw "
"text on the screen."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:82
msgid ""
"Press the \"Add Child Node\" button or \"Other Node\" to create a root node."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:86
msgid "The Create Node dialog opens, showing the long list of available nodes."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:90
msgid "Select the Label node. You can type its name to filter down the list."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:94
msgid ""
"Click on the Label node to select it and click the Create button at the "
"bottom of the window."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:99
msgid ""
"A lot happens when you add a scene's first node. The scene changes to the 2D "
"workspace because Label is a 2D node type. The Label appears, selected, in "
"the top-left corner of the viewport. The node appears in the Scene dock on "
"the left, and the node's properties appear in the Inspector dock on the "
"right."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:105
msgid "Changing a node's properties"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:107
msgid ""
"The next step is to change the Label's \"Text\" property. Let's change it to "
"\"Hello World\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:110
msgid ""
"Head to the Inspector dock on the right of the viewport. Click inside the "
"field below the Text property and type \"Hello World\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:115
msgid "You will see the text draw in the viewport as you type."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:117
msgid ""
"You can move your Label node in the viewport by selecting the move tool in "
"the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:122
msgid ""
"With the Label selected, click and drag anywhere in the viewport to move it "
"to the center of the view delimited by the rectangle."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:128
msgid "Running the scene"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:130
msgid ""
"Everything's ready to run the scene! Press the Play Scene button in the top-"
"right of the screen or press :kbd:`F6` (:kbd:`Cmd + R` on macOS)."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:135
msgid "A popup invites you to save the scene, which is required to run it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:139
msgid ""
"Click the Yes button, and in the file browser that appears, press the Save "
"button to save it as \"Label.tscn\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:144
msgid ""
"The Save Scene As dialog, like other file dialogs in the editor, only allows "
"you to save files inside the project. The ``res://`` path at the top of the "
"window represents the project's root directory and stands for \"resource "
"path\". For more information about file paths in Rebel Engine, see :doc:`/"
"tutorials/scripting/filesystem`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:150
msgid ""
"The application should open in a new window and display the text \"Hello "
"World\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:154
msgid "Close the window or press :kbd:`F8` to quit the running scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:158
msgid ""
"If this doesn't immediately work and you have a hiDPI display on at least "
"one of your monitors, go to Project -> Project Settings -> Display -> Window "
"then enable Allow Hidpi under Dpi."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:163
msgid "Setting the main scene"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:165
msgid ""
"To run our test scene, we used the Play Scene button. Another button next to "
"it allows you to set and run the project's main scene. You can press :kbd:"
"`F5` (:kbd:`Cmd + B` on macOS) to do so."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:171
msgid "A popup window appears and invites you to select the main scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:175
msgid ""
"Click the Select button, and in the file dialog that appears, double click "
"on Label.tscn."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:180
msgid ""
"The demo should run again. Moving forward, every time you run the project, "
"Rebel Engine will use this scene as a starting point."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:183
msgid ""
"The editor saves the main scene's path in a project.rebel file in your "
"project's directory. While you can edit this text file directly to change "
"project settings, you can also use the \"Project -> Project Settings\" "
"window to do so."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/nodes_and_scenes.rst:188
msgid ""
"In the next part, we will discuss another key concept in games and in Rebel "
"Engine: creating instances of a scene."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:17
msgid "Creating your first script"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:19
msgid ""
"In this lesson, you will code your first script to make the Rebel icon turn "
"in circles using GDScript. As we mentioned :ref:`in the introduction <toc-"
"learn-introduction>`, we assume you have programming foundations. The "
"equivalent C# code has been included in another tab for convenience."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:26
msgid ""
"To learn more about GDScript, its keywords, and its syntax, head to the :doc:"
"`GDScript reference</tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics>`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:29
msgid ""
"To learn more about C#, head to the :doc:`C# basics </tutorials/scripting/"
"c_sharp/c_sharp_basics>` page."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:32
#: tutorials/2d/using_tilemaps.rst:19
msgid "Project setup"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:34
msgid ""
"Please create a new project to start with a clean slate. Your project should "
"contain one picture: the Rebel icon, which we often use for prototyping in "
"the community."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:40
msgid ""
"We need to create a Sprite node to display it in the game. In the Scene "
"dock, click the Other Node button."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:45
msgid ""
"Type \"Sprite\" in the search bar to filter nodes and double-click on Sprite "
"to create the node."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:50
msgid "Your Scene tab should now only have a Sprite node."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:54
msgid ""
"A Sprite node needs a texture to display. In the Inspector on the right, you "
"can see that the Texture property says \"[empty]\". To display the Rebel "
"icon, click and drag the file ``icon.png`` from the FileSystem dock onto the "
"Texture slot."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:62
msgid ""
"You can create Sprite nodes automatically by dragging and dropping images on "
"the viewport."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:67
msgid ""
"Then, click and drag the icon in the viewport to center it in the game view."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:72
msgid "Creating a new script"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:74
msgid ""
"To create and attach a new script to our node, right-click on Sprite in the "
"scene dock and select \"Attach Script\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:79
msgid ""
"The Attach Node Script window appears. It allows you to select the script's "
"language and file path, among other options."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:82
msgid ""
"Change the Template from Default to Empty to start with a clean file. Leave "
"the other options by default and click the Create button to create the "
"script."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:87
msgid ""
"The Script workspace should appear with your new ``Sprite.gd`` file open and "
"the following line of code:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:114
msgid ""
"Every GDScript file is implicitly a class. The ``extends`` keyword defines "
"the class this script inherits or extends. In this case, it's ``Sprite``, "
"meaning our script will get access to all the properties and functions of "
"the Sprite node, including classes it extends, like ``Node2D``, "
"``CanvasItem``, and ``Node``."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:120
msgid ""
"In GDScript, if you omit the line with the ``extends`` keyword, your class "
"will implicitly extend :ref:`Reference <class_Reference>`, which Rebel "
"Engine uses to manage your application's memory."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:124
msgid ""
"Inherited properties include the ones you can see in the Inspector dock, "
"like our node's ``texture``."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:129
msgid ""
"By default, the Inspector displays a node's properties in \"Title Case\", "
"with capitalized words separated by a space. In GDScript code, these "
"properties are in \"snake_case\", which is lowercase with words separated by "
"an underscore."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:133
msgid ""
"You can hover any property's name in the Inspector to see a description and "
"its identifier in code."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:137
msgid "Hello, world!"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:139
msgid ""
"Our script currently doesn't do anything. Let's make it print the text "
"\"Hello, world!\" to the Output bottom panel to get started."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:142
msgid "Add the following code to your script:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:158
msgid ""
"Let's break it down. The ``func`` keyword defines a new function named "
"``_init``. This is a special name for our class's constructor. The engine "
"calls ``_init()`` on every object or node upon creating it in memory, if you "
"define this function."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:163
msgid ""
"GDScript is an indent-based language. The tab at the start of the line that "
"says ``print()`` is necessary for the code to work. If you omit it or don't "
"indent a line correctly, the editor will highlight it in red and display the "
"following error message: \"Indented block expected\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:168
msgid ""
"Save the scene if you haven't already, then press :kbd:`F6` (:kbd:`Cmd + R` "
"on macOS) to run it. Look at the **Output** bottom panel that expands. It "
"should display \"Hello, world!\"."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:174
msgid ""
"Delete the ``_init()`` function, so you're only left with the line ``extends "
"Sprite``."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:178
msgid "Turning around"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:180
msgid ""
"It's time to make our node move and rotate. To do so, we're going to add two "
"member variables to our script: the movement speed in pixels per second and "
"the angular speed in radians per second."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:195
msgid ""
"Member variables sit near the top of the script, after any \"extends\" "
"lines, but before functions. Every node instance with this script attached "
"to it will have its own copy of the ``speed`` and ``angular_speed`` "
"properties."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:200
msgid ""
"Angles in Rebel Engine work in radians by default, but you have built-in "
"functions and properties available if you prefer to calculate angles in "
"degrees instead."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:204
msgid ""
"To move our icon, we need to update its position and rotation every frame in "
"the game loop. We can use the ``_process()`` virtual function of the "
"``Node`` class. If you define it in any class that extends the Node class, "
"like Sprite, Rebel Engine will call the function every frame and pass it an "
"argument named ``delta``, the time elapsed since the last frame."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:212
msgid ""
"Games work by rendering many images per second, each called a frame, and "
"they do so in a loop. We measure the rate at which a game produces images in "
"Frames Per Second (FPS). Most games aim for 60 FPS, although you might find "
"figures like 30 FPS on slower mobile devices or 90 to 240 for virtual "
"reality games."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:218
msgid ""
"The engine and game developers do their best to update the game world and "
"render images at a constant time interval, but there are always small "
"variations in frame render times. That's why the engine provides us with "
"this delta time value, making our motion independent of our framerate."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:223
msgid "At the bottom of the script, define the function:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:238
msgid ""
"The ``func`` keyword defines a new function. After it, we have to write the "
"function's name and arguments it takes in parentheses. A colon ends the "
"definition, and the indented blocks that follow are the function's content "
"or instructions."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:243
msgid ""
"Notice how ``_process()``, like ``_init()``, starts with a leading "
"underscore. By convention, Rebel Engine's virtual functions, that is to say, "
"built-in functions you can override to communicate with the engine, start "
"with an underscore."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:248
msgid ""
"The line inside the function, ``rotation += angular_speed * delta``, "
"increments our sprite's rotation every frame. Here, ``rotation`` is a "
"property inherited from the class ``Node2D``, which ``Sprite`` extends. It "
"controls the rotation of our node and works with radians."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:253
msgid ""
"In the code editor, you can ctrl-click on any built-in property or function "
"like ``position``, ``rotation``, or ``_process`` to open the corresponding "
"documentation in a new tab."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:257
msgid "Run the scene to see the Rebel icon turn in-place."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:262
msgid "Moving forward"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:264
msgid ""
"Let's now make the node move. Add the following two lines to the "
"``_process()`` function, ensuring the new lines are indented the same way as "
"the one before them."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:281
msgid ""
"As we already saw, the ``var`` keyword defines a new variable. If you put it "
"at the top of the script, it defines a property of the class. Inside a "
"function, it defines a local variable: it only exists within the function's "
"scope."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:285
msgid ""
"We define a local variable named ``velocity``, a 2D vector representing both "
"a direction and a speed. To make the node move forward, we start from the "
"Vector2 class's constant Vector2.UP, a vector pointing up, and rotate it by "
"calling the ``Vector2.rotated()`` method. This expression, ``Vector2.UP."
"rotated(rotation)``, is a vector pointing forward relative to our icon. "
"Multiplied by our ``speed`` property, it gives us a velocity we can use to "
"move the node forward."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:292
msgid ""
"We add ``velocity * delta`` to the node's ``position`` to move it. The "
"position itself is of type :ref:`Vector2 <class_Vector2>`, a built-in type "
"in Rebel Engine representing a 2D vector."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:296
msgid "Run the scene to see the Rebel icon run in circles."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:300
msgid ""
"Moving a node like that does not take into account colliding with walls or "
"the floor. In :doc:`/getting_started/first_2d_game/index`, you will learn "
"another approach to moving objects while detecting collisions."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:304
msgid ""
"Our node currently moves by itself. In the next part :doc:"
"`scripting_player_input`, we'll use player input to control it."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_first_script.rst:308
#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:111
