#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:5
msgid "Scripting languages"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:7
msgid ""
"This lesson will give you an overview of the available scripting languages "
"in Rebel Engine. You will learn the pros and cons of each option. In the "
"next part, you will write your first script using GDScript."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:11
msgid ""
"**Scripts attach to a node and extend its behavior**. This means that "
"scripts inherit all functions and properties of the node they attach to."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:14
msgid ""
"For example, take a game where a Camera2D node follows a ship. The Camera2D "
"node follows its parent by default. Imagine you want the camera to shake "
"when the player takes damage. As this feature is not built into Rebel "
"Engine, you would attach a script to the Camera2D node and code the shake."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:22
msgid "Available scripting languages"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:24
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine offers **five gameplay programming languages**: GDScript, C#, "
"VisualScript, and, via its GDNative technology, C and C++. There are more :"
"ref:`community-supported languages <tutorials/scripting/gdnative/"
"what_is_gdnative:version compatibility>`, but these are the official ones."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:29
msgid ""
"You can use multiple languages in a single project. For instance, in a team, "
"you could code gameplay logic in GDScript as it's fast to write, let level "
"designers script quests in the graphical language VisualScript, and use C# "
"or C++ to implement complex algorithms and maximize their performance. Or "
"you can write everything in GDScript or C#. It's your call."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:35
msgid ""
"We provide this flexibility to answer the needs of different game projects "
"and developers."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:39
msgid "Which language should I use?"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:41
msgid ""
"If you're a beginner, we recommend to **start with GDScript**. We made this "
"language specifically for Rebel Engine and the needs of game developers. It "
"has a lightweight and straightforward syntax and provides the tightest "
"integration with Rebel Engine."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:48
msgid ""
"For C#, you will need an external code editor like `VSCode <https://code."
"visualstudio.com/>`__ or Visual Studio. While C# support is now mature, you "
"will find fewer learning resources for it compared to GDScript. That's why "
"we recommend C# mainly to users who already have experience with the "
"language."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:54
msgid "Let's look at each language's features, as well as its pros and cons."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:59
msgid ""
":doc:`GDScript</tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics>` is an `object-"
"oriented <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming>`__ and "
"`imperative <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperative_programming>`__ "
"programming language built for Rebel Engine. It's made by and for game "
"developers to save you time coding games. Its features include:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:65
msgid "A simple syntax that leads to short files."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:66
msgid "Blazing fast compilation and loading times."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:67
msgid ""
"Tight editor integration, with code completion for nodes, signals, and more "
"information from the scene it's attached to."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:69
msgid ""
"Built-in vector and transform types, making it efficient for heavy use of "
"linear algebra, a must for games."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:71
msgid "Supports multiple threads as efficiently as statically typed languages."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:72
msgid ""
"No `garbage collection <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Garbage_collection_(computer_science)>`__, as this feature eventually gets "
"in the way when creating games. The engine counts references and manages the "
"memory for you in most cases by default, but you can also control memory if "
"you need to."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:77
msgid ""
"`Gradual typing <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradual_typing>`__. Variables "
"have dynamic types by default, but you also can use type hints for strong "
"type checks."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:81
msgid ""
"GDScript looks like Python as you structure your code blocks using "
"indentations, but it doesn't work the same way in practice. It's inspired by "
"multiple languages, including Squirrel, Lua, and Python."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:87
msgid "Why don't we use Python or Lua directly?"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:89
msgid ""
"Integartion of a language takes a lot of work and there are severe "
"limitations. For example, it's difficult to provide threading support with "
"Python."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:92
msgid ""
"Developing a dedicated language doesn't require more work and we can tailor "
"it to game developers' needs. We're also able to address performance "
"optimizations and features that would be difficult to offer with third-party "
"languages."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:98
msgid ".NET / C#"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:100
msgid ""
"As Microsoft's `C# <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"C_Sharp_(programming_language)>`__ is a favorite amongst game developers, we "
"officially support it. C# is a mature and flexible language with tons of "
"libraries written for it. We could add support for it thanks to a generous "
"donation from Microsoft."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:108
msgid ""
"C# offers a good tradeoff between performance and ease of use, although you "
"should be aware of its garbage collector."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:111
msgid "You must use the Mono edition of the Rebel Editor to script in C#."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:113
msgid ""
"Since Rebel Engine uses the `Mono <https://www.mono-project.com/>`__ .NET "
"runtime, in theory, you can use any third-party .NET library or framework in "
"Rebel Engine, as well as any Common Language Infrastructure-compliant "
"programming language, such as F#, Boo, or ClojureCLR. However, C# is the "
"only officially supported .NET option."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:118
msgid ""
"GDScript code itself doesn't execute as fast as compiled C# or C++. However, "
"most script code calls functions written with fast algorithms in C++ code "
"inside the engine. In many cases, writing gameplay logic in GDScript, C#, or "
"C++ won't have a significant impact on performance."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:125
#: tutorials/scripting/visual_script/index.rst:2
msgid "VisualScript"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:127
msgid ""
":doc:`Visual Scripting</tutorials/scripting/visual_script/"
"what_is_visual_scripting>` is a graph-based visual programming language "
"where you connect blocks. It can be a great tool for non-programmers like "
"game designers and artists."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:133
msgid ""
"You can use other languages to create custom blocks that are specific to "
"your game, for example, to script AIs, quests, or dialogues. That's where "
"the strength of VisualScript lies."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:137
msgid ""
"While it provides all the basic building blocks you need to code complete "
"games, we do not recommend to use VisualScript this way. Programming "
"everything with it is slow compared to using other programming languages."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:143
msgid ""
"For more information, see :doc:`Getting started with VisualScript </"
"tutorials/scripting/visual_script/getting_started>`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:147
msgid "C and C++ via GDNative"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:149
msgid ""
"GDNative allows you to write game code in C or C++ without needing to "
"recompile or even restart the Rebel Editor."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:154
msgid ""
"You can use any version of the language or mix compiler brands and versions "
"for the generated shared libraries, thanks to our use of an internal C API "
"Bridge."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:157
msgid ""
"GDNative is the best choice for performance. You don't need to use it "
"throughout an entire game, as you can write other parts in GDScript, C#, or "
"VisualScript."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:161
msgid ""
"When working with GDNative, the available types, functions, and properties "
"closely resemble Rebel Engine's actual C++ API."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:167
msgid ""
"Scripts are files containing code that you attach to a node to extend its "
"functionality."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:170
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine supports five official scripting languages, offering you "
"flexibility between performance and ease of use."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_languages.rst:173
msgid ""
"You can mix languages, for instance, to implement demanding algorithms with "
"C or C++ and write most of the game logic with GDScript or C#."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:6
msgid "Listening to player input"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:8
msgid ""
"Building upon the previous lesson :doc:`scripting_first_script`, let's look "
"at another important feature of any game: giving control to the player. To "
"add this, we need to modify our ``Sprite.gd`` code."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:14
msgid "You have two main tools to process the player's input in Rebel Engine:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:16
msgid ""
"The built-in input callbacks, mainly ``_unhandled_input()``. Like "
"``_process()``, it's a built-in virtual function that Rebel Engine calls "
"every time the player presses a key. It's the tool you want to use to react "
"to events that don't happen every frame, like pressing :kbd:`Space` to jump. "
"To learn more about input callbacks, see :doc:`/tutorials/inputs/inputevent`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:21
msgid ""
"The ``Input`` singleton. A singleton is a globally accessible object. Rebel "
"Engine provides access to several in scripts. It's the right tool to check "
"for input every frame."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:25
msgid ""
"We're going to use the ``Input`` singleton here as we need to know if the "
"player wants to turn or move every frame."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:28
msgid ""
"For turning, we should use a new variable: ``direction``. In our "
"``_process()`` function, replace the ``rotation += angular_speed * delta`` "
"line with the code below."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:57
msgid ""
"Our ``direction`` local variable is a multiplier representing the direction "
"in which the player wants to turn. A value of ``0`` means the player isn't "
"pressing the left or the right arrow key. A value of ``1`` means the player "
"wants to turn right, and ``-1`` means they want to turn left."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:62
msgid ""
"To produce these values, we introduce conditions and the use of ``Input``. A "
"condition starts with the ``if`` keyword in GDScript and ends with a colon. "
"The condition is the expression between the keyword and the end of the line."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:66
msgid ""
"To check if a key was pressed this frame, we call ``Input."
"is_action_pressed()``. The method takes a text string representing an input "
"action and returns ``true`` if the action is pressed, ``false`` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:70
msgid ""
"The two actions we use above, \"ui_left\" and \"ui_right\", are predefined "
"in every Rebel project. They respectively trigger when the player presses "
"the left and right arrows on the keyboard or left and right on a gamepad's D-"
"pad."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:74
msgid ""
"You can see and edit input actions in your project by going to Project -> "
"Project Settings and clicking on the Input Map tab."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:77
msgid ""
"Finally, we use the ``direction`` as a multiplier when we update the node's "
"``rotation``: ``rotation += angular_speed * direction * delta``."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:80
msgid ""
"If you run the scene with this code, the icon should rotate when you press :"
"kbd:`Left` and :kbd:`Right`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:84
msgid "Moving when pressing \"up\""
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:86
msgid ""
"To only move when pressing a key, we need to modify the code that calculates "
"the velocity. Replace the line starting with ``var velocity`` with the code "
"below."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:104
msgid ""
"We initialize the ``velocity`` with a value of ``Vector2.ZERO``, another "
"constant of the built-in ``Vector`` type representing a 2D vector of length "
"0."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:107
msgid ""
"If the player presses the \"ui_up\" action, we then update the velocity's "
"value, causing the sprite to move forward."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:172
msgid ""
"If you run the scene, you should now be able to rotate with the left and "
"right arrow keys and move forward by pressing :kbd:`Up`."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:180
msgid ""
"In summary, every script in Rebel Engine represents a class and extends one "
"of the engine's built-in classes. The node types your classes inherit from "
"give you access to properties like ``rotation`` and ``position`` in our "
"sprite's case. You also inherit many functions, which we didn't get to use "
"in this example."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:185
msgid ""
"In GDScript, the variables you put at the top of the file are your class's "
"properties, also called member variables. Besides variables, you can define "
"functions, which, for the most part, will be your classes' methods."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:189
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine provides several virtual functions you can define to connect "
"your class with the engine. These include ``_process()``, to apply changes "
"to the node every frame, and ``_unhandled_input()``, to receive input events "
"like key and button presses from the users. There are quite a few more."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:194
msgid ""
"The ``Input`` singleton allows you to react to the players' input anywhere "
"in your code. In particular, you'll get to use it in the ``_process()`` loop."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/scripting_player_input.rst:197
msgid ""
"In the next lesson :doc:`signals`, we'll build upon the relationship between "
"scripts and nodes by having our nodes trigger code in scripts."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:-1
#: tutorials/scripting/instancing_with_signals.rst:-1
msgid "Signal"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:10
msgid "Using signals"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:12
msgid ""
"In this lesson, we will look at signals. They are messages that nodes emit "
"when something specific happens to them, like a button being pressed. Other "
"nodes can connect to that signal and call a function when the event occurs."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:16
msgid ""
"Signals are a delegation mechanism built into Rebel Engine that allows one "
"game object to react to a change in another without them referencing one "
"another. Using signals limits `coupling <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Coupling_(computer_programming)>`__ and keeps your code flexible."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:22
msgid ""
"For example, you might have a life bar on the screen that represents the "
"player’s health. When the player takes damage or uses a healing potion, you "
"want the bar to reflect the change. To do so, in Rebel Engine, you would use "
"signals."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:26
msgid ""
"As mentioned in the introduction, signals are Rebel Engine's version of the "
"observer pattern. You can learn more about it here: https://"
"gameprogrammingpatterns.com/observer.html"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:30
msgid ""
"We will now use a signal to make our Rebel icon from the previous lesson (:"
"doc:`scripting_player_input`) move and stop by pressing a button."
msgstr ""

