#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:307
msgid "Custom signals"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:309
msgid ""
"This section is a reference on how to define and use your own signals, and "
"does not build upon the project created in previous lessons."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:312
msgid ""
"You can define custom signals in a script. Say, for example, that you want "
"to show a game over screen when the player's health reaches zero. To do so, "
"you could define a signal named \"died\" or \"health_depleted\" when their "
"health reaches 0."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:326
msgid ""
"As signals represent events that just occurred, we generally use an action "
"verb in the past tense in their names."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:329
msgid ""
"Your signals work the same way as built-in ones: they appear in the Node tab "
"and you can connect to them like any other."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:334
msgid "To emit a signal in your scripts, call ``emit_signal()``."
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:344
msgid ""
"A signal can optionally declare one or more arguments. Specify the argument "
"names between parentheses:"
msgstr ""

#: getting_started/step_by_step/signals.rst:356
msgid ""
"The signal arguments show up in the editor's node dock, and Rebel Engine can "
"use them to generate callback functions for you. However, you can still emit "
"any number of arguments when you emit signals. So it's up to you to emit the "
"correct values."
