#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:2
msgid "Importing audio samples"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:5
msgid "Supported files"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:7
msgid ""
"The Rebel Editor provides three options to import your audio data: WAV, Ogg "
"Vorbis and MP3."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:10
msgid "Each has different advantages."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:12
msgid ""
"WAV files use raw data or light compression (IMA-ADPCM). They are "
"lightweight on the CPU to play back (hundreds of simultaneous voices in this "
"format are fine). The downside is that they take up a lot of disk space."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:15
msgid ""
"Ogg Vorbis files use a stronger compression that results in much smaller "
"file size, but require significantly more processing power to play back."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:18
msgid ""
"MP3 files use better compression than WAV with IMA-ADPCM, but worse than Ogg "
"Vorbis. This means that a MP3 file with roughly equal quality to Ogg Vorbis "
"will be significantly larger. On the bright side, MP3 requires less CPU "
"usage to play back compared to Ogg Vorbis. This makes MP3 useful for mobile "
"and HTML5 projects where CPU resources are limited, especially when playing "
"multiple compressed sounds at the same time (such as long ambient sounds)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:26
msgid "Here is a comparative chart."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:29
msgid "Format"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:29
msgid "1 second of audio"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:31
msgid "WAV 24-bit, 96 kHz, stereo"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:31
msgid "576 KB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:33
msgid "WAV 16-bit, 44 kHz, mono"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:33
msgid "88 KB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:35
msgid "WAV 16-bit, IMA-ADPCM, mono"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:35
msgid "22 KB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:37
msgid "MP3 192 Kb/s, stereo"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:37
msgid "24 KB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:39
msgid "Ogg Vorbis 128 Kb/s, stereo"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:39
msgid "16 KB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:41
msgid "Ogg Vorbis 96 Kb/s, stereo"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:41
msgid "12 KB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:44
msgid ""
"Consider using WAV for short and repetitive sound effects, and Ogg Vorbis "
"for music, speech, and long sound effects."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:48
#: tutorials/best_practices/index.rst:2
msgid "Best practices"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:50
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine has an :doc:`extensive bus system </tutorials/audio/"
"audio_buses>` with built-in effects. This saves SFX artists the need to add "
"reverb to the sound effects, reducing their size greatly and ensuring "
"correct trimming. Say no to SFX with baked reverb!"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:57
msgid "As you can see above, sound effects become huge with reverb added."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:60
msgid "Trimming"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:62
msgid ""
"One issue that happens often is that the waveform is exported with long "
"silences at the beginning and at the end. These are inserted by DAWs when "
"saving to a waveform, increase their size unnecessarily and add latency to "
"the moment they are played back."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:67
msgid "Importing as WAV with the **Trimming** option enabled solves this."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:71
msgid "Looping"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:73
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine supports looping in the samples (tools such as Sound Forge or "
"Audition can add loop points to WAV files). This is useful for sound "
"effects, such as engines, machine guns etc. Ping-pong looping is also "
"supported."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_audio_samples.rst:78
msgid ""
"As an alternative, the Import dock has a **Loop** option that enables "
"looping for the entire sample when importing."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:2
msgid "Importing images"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:5
msgid "Supported image formats"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:7
msgid "The Rebel Editor can import the following image formats:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:9
msgid ""
"BMP (``.bmp``) - No support for 16-bit per pixel images. Only 1-bit, 4-bit, "
"8-bit, 24-bit, and 32-bit per pixel images are supported."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:11
msgid ""
"DirectDraw Surface (``.dds``) - If mipmaps are present in the texture, they "
"will be loaded directly. This can be used to achieve effects using custom "
"mipmaps."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:14
msgid ""
"OpenEXR (``.exr``) - Supports HDR (highly recommended for panorama skies)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:16
msgid ""
"Radiance HDR (``.hdr``) - Supports HDR (highly recommended for panorama "
"skies)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:18
msgid ""
"JPEG (``.jpg``, ``.jpeg``) - Doesn't support transparency per the format's "
"limitations."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:20
msgid ""
"PNG (``.png``) - Precision is limited to 8 bits per channel upon importing "
"(no HDR images)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:22
msgid "Truevision Targa (``.tga``)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:23
msgid ""
"SVG (``.svg``, ``.svgz``) - SVGs are rasterized using `NanoSVG <https://"
"github.com/memononen/nanosvg>`__ when importing them. Support is limited; "
"complex vectors may not render correctly. For complex vectors, rendering "
"them to PNGs using Inkscape is often a better solution. This can be "
"automated thanks to its `command-line interface <https://wiki.inkscape.org/"
"wiki/index.php/Using_the_Command_Line#Export_files>`__."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:28
msgid ""
"WebP (``.webp``) - WebP files support transparency and can be compressed "
"lossily or losslessly. The precision is limited to 8 bits per channel."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:34
msgid ""
"If you've compiled the Rebel Editor from source with specific modules "
"disabled, some formats may not be available."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:38
msgid "Importing textures"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:40
msgid ""
"The default action in Rebel Editor is to import images as textures. Textures "
"are stored in video memory and can't be accessed directly. This is what "
"makes drawing them efficient."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:44
msgid "Import options are vast:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:49
msgid "Detect 3D"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:51
msgid ""
"This option makes Rebel Engine be aware of when a texture (which is imported "
"for 2D as default) is used in 3D. If this happens, setting are changed so "
"the texture flags are friendlier to 3D (mipmaps, filter and repeat become "
"enabled and compression is changed to VRAM). Texture is also reimported "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:55
msgid "Compression"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:57
msgid ""
"Images are one of the largest assets in a game. To handle them efficiently, "
"they need to be compressed. Rebel Engine offers several compression methods, "
"depending on the use case."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:61
msgid "Compress Mode"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:63
msgid ""
"**VRAM Compression:** This is the most common compression mode for 3D "
"assets. Size on disk is reduced and video memory usage is also decreased "
"considerably (usually by a factor between 4 and 6). This mode should be "
"avoided for 2D as it exhibits noticeable artifacts."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:67
msgid ""
"**Lossless Compression:** This is the most common compression mode for 2D "
"assets. It shows assets without any kind of artifacting, and disk "
"compression is decent. It will use considerably more amount of video memory "
"than VRAM Compression, though. This is also the recommended setting for "
"pixel art."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:71
msgid ""
"**Lossy Compression:** This is a good choice for large 2D assets. It has "
"some artifacts, but less than VRAM and the file size is several times lower "
"compared to Lossless or Uncompressed. Video memory usage isn't decreased by "
"this mode; it's the same as with Lossless Compression or Uncompressed."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:75
msgid ""
"**Uncompressed:** Only useful for formats that can't be compressed (such as "
"raw float images)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:78
msgid ""
"In this table, each of the four options are described together with their "
"advantages and disadvantages (|good| = best, |bad| = worst):"
msgstr ""

