#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:104
msgid "HDR Mode"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:106
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine supports high dynamic range textures (as .HDR or .EXR). These "
"are mostly useful as high dynamic range equirectangular panorama skies (the "
"internet has plenty if you search for them). Modern PCs support the BC6H "
"VRAM format, but there are still plenty that do not."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:109
msgid ""
"If you want Rebel Engine to ensure full compatibility in terms of the kind "
"of textures, enable the \"Force RGBE\" option."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:112
msgid "Normal Map"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:114
msgid ""
"When using a texture as normal map, only the red and green channels are "
"required. Given regular texture compression algorithms produce artifacts "
"that don't look that nice in normal maps, the RGTC compression format is the "
"best fit for this data. Forcing this option to \"Enabled\" will make Rebel "
"Editor import the image as RGTC compressed. By default, it's set to "
"\"Detect\", which means that if the texture is ever used as a normal map, it "
"will be changed to \"Enabled\" and reimported automatically."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:119
msgid ""
"Note that RGTC compression affects the resulting normal map image. You will "
"have to adjust custom shaders that use the normal map to take this into "
"account."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:135
msgid ""
"There are plenty of settings that can be toggled when importing an image as "
"a texture, depending on the use case."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:138
msgid "Repeat"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:140
msgid ""
"This setting is most commonly used in 3D, and is therefore generally "
"disabled in 2D. It sets UV coordinates going beyond the 0,0 - 1,1 range to "
"\"loop\"."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:142
msgid "Repeating can optionally be set to mirrored mode."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:145
#: tutorials/audio/audio_buses.rst:185
msgid "Filter"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:147
msgid ""
"When pixels become larger than the screen pixels, this option enables linear "
"interpolation for them. The result is a smoother (less blocky) texture. This "
"setting can be commonly used in 2D and 3D, but it's usually disabled when "
"making pixel perfect games."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:150
msgid "Mipmaps"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:152
msgid ""
"When pixels become smaller than the screen, mipmaps kick in. This helps "
"reduce the grainy effect when shrinking the textures. Keep in mind that, in "
"older hardware (GLES2, mainly mobile), there are some requirements to use "
"mipmaps:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:155
msgid "Texture width and height must be powers of 2"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:156
msgid "Repeat must be enabled"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:158
msgid ""
"Keep in mind the above when making phone games and applications, want to aim "
"for full compatibility, and need mipmaps."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:160
msgid ""
"When doing 3D, mipmap should be turned on, as this also improves performance "
"(smaller versions of the texture are used for objects further away)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:163
msgid "Anisotropic"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:165
msgid ""
"When textures are near parallel to the view (like floors), this option makes "
"them have more detail by reducing blurriness."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:168
msgid "sRGB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:170
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine uses Linear colorspace when rendering 3D. Textures mapped to "
"albedo or detail channels need to have this option turned on in order for "
"colors to look correct. When set to **Detect** mode, the texture will be "
"marked as sRGB when used in albedo channels."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:175
msgid ""
"Since the texture will have its data modified when sRGB is enabled, this "
"means using the same texture in both 2D and 3D will make the texture display "
"with incorrect colors in either 2D or 3D."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:179
msgid ""
"To work around this, make a copy of the texture on the filesystem and enable "
"sRGB on one of the copies only. Use the copy with sRGB enabled in 3D, and "
"the copy with sRGB disabled in 2D."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:184
msgid "Process"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:186
msgid ""
"Some special processes can be applied to images when imported as textures."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:189
msgid "Fix Alpha Border"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:191
msgid ""
"This puts pixels of the same surrounding color in transition from "
"transparency to non transparency. It helps mitigate the outline effect when "
"exporting images from Photoshop and the like."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:196
msgid ""
"It's a good idea to leave it on by default, unless specific values are "
"needed."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:199
msgid "Premultiplied Alpha"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:201
msgid ""
"An alternative to fix darkened borders is to use premultiplied alpha. By "
"enabling this option, the texture will be converted to this format. Keep in "
"mind that a material will need to be created that uses the PREMULT ALPHA "
"blend mode on canvas items that need it."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:205
msgid "HDR as sRGB"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:207
msgid ""
"A few HDR files are broken and contain sRGB color data. It is advised not to "
"use them, but, in the worst-case scenario, toggling this option on will make "
"them look right."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:210
msgid "Invert Color"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:212
msgid ""
"Reverses the image's color. This is useful, for example, to convert a height "
"map generated by external programs to depth map to use with :doc:`/"
"tutorials/3d/spatial_material`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:215
msgid "Svg"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_images.rst:220
msgid ""
"This option only applies to SVG files. It controls the scale of the SVG "
"image. The default scale (1.0) will make the imported SVG match its original "
"design scale."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:2
msgid "Importing 3D scenes"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:5
msgid "Rebel Editor scene importer"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:7
msgid ""
"When dealing with 3D assets, Rebel Editor has a flexible and configurable "
"importer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:9
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine works with *scenes*. This means that the entire scene being "
"worked on in your favorite 3D DCC will be transferred as close as possible."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:12
msgid "Rebel Engine supports the following 3D *scene file formats*:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:14
msgid ""
"glTF 2.0 **(recommended)**. Rebel Engine has full support for both text (``."
"gltf``) and binary (``.glb``) formats."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:15
msgid "DAE (COLLADA), an older format that is fully supported."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:16
msgid ""
"OBJ (Wavefront) format + their MTL material files. This is also fully "
"supported, but pretty limited (no support for pivots, skeletons, animations, "
"PBR materials, …)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:17
msgid ""
"FBX, supported via the Open Asset Import library. However, FBX is "
"proprietary, so we recommend using other formats listed above, if suitable "
"for your workflow."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:20
msgid ""
"Just copy the scene file together with the texture to the project "
"repository, and Rebel Editor will do a full import."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:22
msgid ""
"It is important that the mesh is not deformed by bones when exporting. Make "
"sure that the skeleton is reset to its T-pose or default rest pose before "
"exporting with your favorite 3D editor."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:26
msgid "Exporting DAE files from Maya and 3DS Max"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:28
msgid ""
"Autodesk added built-in COLLADA support to Maya and 3DS Max, but it's broken "
"by default and should not be used. The best way to export this format is by "
"using the `OpenCollada <https://github.com/KhronosGroup/OpenCOLLADA/wiki/"
"OpenCOLLADA-Tools>`__ plugins. They work well, although they are not always "
"up-to date with the latest version of the software."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:36
msgid "Exporting glTF 2.0 files from Blender"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:38
msgid ""
"There are three ways to export glTF files from Blender. As a glTF binary (``."
"glb`` file), glTF embedded (``.gltf`` file), and with textures (``gltf`` + "
"``.bin`` + textures)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:41
msgid ""
"glTF binary files are the smallest of the three options. They include the "
"mesh and textures set up in Blender. When brought into Rebel Editor the "
"textures are part of the object's material file."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:44
msgid ""
"glTF embedded files function the same way as binary files. They don't "
"provide extra functionality in Rebel Engine, and shouldn't be used since "
"they have a larger file size."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:47
msgid ""
"There are two reasons to use glTF with the textures separate. One is to have "
"the scene description in a text based format and the binary data in a "
"separate binary file. This can be useful for version control if you want to "
"review changes in a text based format. The second is you need the texture "
"files separate from the material file. If you don't need either of those "
"glTF binary files are fine."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:54
msgid ""
"Blender versions older than 3.2 do not export emissive textures with the "
"glTF file. If your model uses one and you're using an older version of "
"Blender, it must be brought in separately."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:66
msgid "Exporting DAE files from Blender"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:68
msgid ""
"Blender has built-in COLLADA support, but it does not work properly for the "
"needs of game engines and should not be used as is."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:72
msgid "Exporting textures separately"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:74
msgid ""
"While textures can be exported with a model in certain file formats, such as "
"glTF 2.0, you can also export them separately. Rebel Engine uses PBR "
"(physically based rendering) for its materials, so if a texturing program "
"can export PBR textures, they can work in Rebel Engine. This includes the "
"`Substance suite <https://www.adobe.com/creativecloud/3d-ar.html>`__, "
"`ArmorPaint (open source) <https://armorpaint.org/>`__, and `Material Maker "
"(open source) <https://github.com/RodZill4/material-maker>`__."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:79
msgid ""
"For more information on Rebel Engine's materials, see :doc:`/tutorials/3d/"
"spatial_material`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:82
msgid "Exporting considerations"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:84
msgid ""
"Since GPUs can only render triangles, meshes that contain quads or N-gons "
"have to be *triangulated* before they can be rendered. Rebel Engine can "
"triangulate meshes on import, but results may be unpredictable or incorrect, "
"especially with N-gons. Regardless of the target application, triangulating "
"*before* exporting the scene will lead to more consistent results and should "
"be done whenever possible."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:91
msgid ""
"To avoid issues with incorrect triangulation after importing in Rebel "
"Editor, it is recommended to make the 3D DCC triangulate objects on its own. "
"In Blender, this can be done by adding a Triangulate modifier to your "
"objects and making sure **Apply Modifiers** is checked in the export dialog. "
"Alternatively, depending on the exporter, you may be able to find and enable "
"a **Triangulate Faces** option in the export dialog."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:98
msgid ""
"To avoid issues with 3D selection in the editor, it is recommended to apply "
"the object transform in the 3D DCC before exporting the scene."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:102
msgid "Import workflows"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:104
msgid ""
"Rebel Editor scene importer allows different workflows regarding how data is "
"imported. Depending on many options, it is possible to import a scene with:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:107
msgid ""
"External materials (default): Where each material is saved to a file "
"resource. Modifications to them are kept."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:108
msgid ""
"External meshes: Where each mesh is saved to a different file. Many users "
"prefer to deal with meshes directly."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:109
msgid ""
"External animations: Allowing saved animations to be modified and merged "
"when sources change."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:110
msgid ""
"External scenes: Save each of the root nodes of the imported scenes as a "
"separate scene."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:111
msgid "Single scene: A single scene file with everything built in."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:115
msgid ""
"As different developers have different needs, this import process is highly "
"customizable."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:118
msgid "Import options"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:120
msgid "The importer has several options, which will be discussed below:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:128
msgid "Root Type"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:130
msgid ""
"By default, the type of the root node in imported scenes is \"Spatial\", but "
"this can be modified."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:133
msgid "Root Name"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:135
msgid "Allows setting a specific name to the generated root node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:138
msgid "Root Scale"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:140
msgid "The scale of the root node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:143
msgid "Custom Script"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:145
msgid ""
"A special script to process the whole scene after import can be provided. "
"This is great for post processing, changing materials, doing funny stuff "
"with the geometry etc."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:149
msgid "Create a script like this:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:161
msgid ""
"The ``post_import`` function takes the imported scene as argument (the "
"parameter is actually the root node of the scene). The scene that will "
"finally be used must be returned. It can be a different one."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:166
msgid "Storing Nodes"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:168
msgid ""
"By default, Rebel Editor imports a single scene. This option allows "
"specifying that nodes below the root will each be a separate scene and "
"instanced into the imported one."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:172
msgid ""
"Of course, instancing such imported scenes in other places manually works, "
"too."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:179
#: tutorials/export/exporting_for_macos.rst:113 tutorials/io/data_paths.rst:43
#: tutorials/io/data_paths.rst:71
msgid "Location"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:181
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine supports materials in meshes or nodes. By default, materials "
"will be put on each node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:185
msgid "Storing Materials"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:187
msgid ""
"Materials can be stored within the scene or in external files. By default, "
"they are stored in external files so editing them is possible. This is "
"because most 3D DCCs don't have the same material options as those present "
"in Rebel Engine."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:191
msgid ""
"When materials are built-in, they will be lost each time the source scene is "
"modified and re-imported."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:196
msgid ""
"Rebel Editor will not reimport materials that are stored in external files "
"unless you remove the associated ``.material`` file before reimporting."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:199
msgid ""
"To force reimporting materials every time the 3D scene is reimported, change "
"the material storage mode in the 3D scene by selecting it in the FileSystem "
"dock, going to the Import dock then setting **Material > Storage** to "
"**Built-In** instead of **Files**."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:205
msgid "Keep On Reimport"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:207
msgid ""
"Once materials are edited to use Rebel Engine features, the importer will "
"keep the edited ones and ignore the ones coming from the source scene. This "
"option is only present if materials are saved as files."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:212
msgid "Meshes"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:215
msgid "Compress"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:217
msgid ""
"Makes meshes use less precise numbers for multiple aspects of the mesh in "
"order to save space."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:220
msgid "These are:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:221
msgid ""
"Transform Matrix (Location, rotation, and scale)             : 32-bit float "
"to 16-bit signed integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:222
msgid ""
"Vertices                                                     : 32-bit float "
"to 16-bit signed integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:223
msgid ""
"Normals                                                      : 32-bit float "
"to 32-bit unsigned integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:224
msgid ""
"Tangents                                                     : 32-bit float "
"to 32-bit unsigned integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:225
msgid ""
"Vertex Colors                                                : 32-bit float "
"to 32-bit unsigned integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:226
msgid ""
"UV                                                           : 32-bit float "
"to 32-bit unsigned integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:227
msgid ""
"UV2                                                          : 32-bit float "
"to 32-bit unsigned integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:228
msgid ""
"Vertex weights                                               : 32-bit float "
"to 16-bit unsigned integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:229
msgid ""
"Armature bones                                               : 32-bit float "
"to 16-bit unsigned integer."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:230
msgid ""
"Array index                                                  : 32-bit or 16-"
"bit unsigned integer based on how many elements there are."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:232
msgid "Additional info:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:233
msgid ""
"UV2 = The second UV channel for detail textures and baked lightmap textures."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:234
msgid ""
"Array index = An array of numbers that number each element of the arrays "
"above; i.e. they number the vertices and normals."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:236
msgid ""
"In some cases, this might lead to loss of precision, so disabling this "
"option may be needed. For instance, if a mesh is very big or there are "
"multiple meshes being imported that cover a large area, compressing the "
"import of this mesh(es) may lead to gaps in geometry or vertices not being "
"exactly where they should be."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:242
msgid "Ensure Tangents"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:244
msgid ""
"If textures with normal mapping are to be used, meshes need to have tangent "
"arrays. This option ensures that these are generated if not present in the "
"source scene. Rebel Engine uses `Mikktspace <http://www.mikktspace.com/>`__ "
"for this, but it's always better to have them generated in the exporter."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:250
msgid "Storing Meshes"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:252
msgid ""
"Meshes can be stored in separate files (resources) instead of built-in. This "
"does not have much practical use unless one wants to build objects with them "
"directly."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:255
msgid ""
"This option is provided to help those who prefer working directly with "
"meshes instead of scenes."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:259
msgid "Light Baking"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:261
msgid "Whether or not the mesh is used in baked lightmaps."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:263
msgid "**Disabled:** The mesh is not used in baked lightmaps."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:264
msgid "**Enable:** The mesh is used in baked lightmaps."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:265
msgid ""
"**Gen Lightmaps:** The mesh is used in baked lightmaps, and unwraps a second "
"UV layer for lightmaps."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:267
msgid ""
"For more information on light baking see :doc:`/tutorials/3d/"
"baked_lightmaps`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:270
msgid "External Files"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:272
msgid ""
"Generated meshes and materials can be optionally stored in a subdirectory "
"with the name of the scene."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:276
msgid "Animation options"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:278
msgid ""
"Rebel Editor provides many options regarding how animation data is dealt "
"with. Some exporters (such as Blender) can generate many animations in a "
"single file. Others, such as 3DS Max or Maya, need many animations put into "
"the same timeline or, at worst, put each animation in a separate file."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:285
msgid "Import of animations is enabled by default."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:289
msgid ""
"To modify animations from an imported 3D scene, you need to change the "
"animation storage option from **Built-In** to **Files** in the Import dock. "
"Otherwise, changes made to animations in Rebel Editor will be lost when the "
"project is run."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:294
msgid "FPS"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:296
msgid ""
"Most 3D export formats store animation timeline in seconds instead of "
"frames. To ensure animations are imported as faithfully as possible, please "
"specify the frames per second used to edit them. Failing to do this may "
"result in shaky animations."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:301
msgid "Filter Script"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:303
msgid ""
"It is possible to specify a filter script in a special syntax to decide "
"which tracks from which animations should be kept."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:306
msgid ""
"The filter script is executed against each imported animation. The syntax "
"consists of two types of statements, the first for choosing which animations "
"to filter, and the second for filtering individual tracks within the matched "
"animation. All name patterns are performed using a case insensitive "
"expression match, using ``?`` and ``*`` wildcards (using ``String.matchn()`` "
"under the hood)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:312
msgid ""
"The script must start with an animation filter statement (as denoted by the "
"line beginning with an ``@``). For example, if we would like to apply "
"filters to all imported animations which have a name ending in "
"``\"_Loop\"``::"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:318
msgid ""
"Similarly, additional patterns can be added to the same line, separated by "
"commas. Here is a modified example to additionally *include* all animations "
"with names that begin with ``\"Arm_Left\"``, but also *exclude* all "
"animations which have names ending in ``\"Attack\"``::"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:324
msgid ""
"Following the animation selection filter statement, we add track filtering "
"patterns to indicate which animation tracks should be kept or discarded. If "
"no track filter patterns are specified, then all tracks within the matched "
"animations will be discarded!"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:328
msgid ""
"It's important to note that track filter statements are applied in order for "
"each track within the animation, this means that one line may include a "
"track, a later rule can still discard it. Similarly, a track excluded by an "
"early rule may then be re-included once again by a filter rule further down "
"in the filter script."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:333
msgid ""
"For example: include all tracks in animations with names ending in "
"``\"_Loop\"``, but discard any tracks affecting a ``\"Skeleton\"`` which end "
"in ``\"Control\"``, unless they have ``\"Arm\"`` in their name::"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:342
msgid ""
"In the above example, tracks like ``\"Skeleton:Leg_Control\"`` would be "
"discarded, while tracks such as ``\"Skeleton:Head\"`` or ``\"Skeleton:"
"Arm_Left_Control\"`` would be retained."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:345
msgid ""
"Any track filter lines that do not begin with a ``+`` or ``-`` are ignored."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:348
msgid "Storing Animations"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:350
msgid ""
"By default, animations are saved as built-in. It is possible to save them to "
"a file instead. This allows adding custom tracks to the animations and "
"keeping them after a reimport."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:354
msgid "Optimizer"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:356
msgid ""
"When animations are imported, an optimizer is run, which reduces the size of "
"the animation considerably. In general, this should always be turned on "
"unless you suspect that an animation might be broken due to it being enabled."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:360
msgid "Clips"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:362
msgid ""
"It is possible to specify multiple animations from a single timeline as "
"clips. For this to work, the model must have only one animation that is "
"named ``default``. To create clips, change the clip amount to something "
"greater than zero. You can then name a clip, specify which frames it starts "
"and stops on, and choose whether the animation loops or not."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:368
msgid "Scene inheritance"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:370
msgid ""
"In many cases, it may be desired to make modifications to the imported "
"scene. By default, this is not possible because if the source asset changes "
"(source ``.dae``, ``.gltf``, ``.obj`` file re-exported from 3D modelling "
"app), Rebel Editor will re-import the whole scene."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:373
msgid ""
"It is possible, however, to make local modifications by using *Scene "
"Inheritance*. Try to open the imported scene and the following dialog will "
"appear:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:378
msgid "In inherited scenes, the only limitations for modifications are:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:380
msgid "Nodes can't be removed (but can be added anywhere)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:381
msgid ""
"Sub-Resources can't be edited (save them externally as described above for "
"this)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:383
msgid "Other than that, everything is allowed!"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:386
msgid "Import hints"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:388
msgid ""
"Many times, when editing a scene, there are common tasks that need to be "
"done after exporting:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:390
msgid "Adding collision detection to objects."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:391
msgid "Setting objects as navigation meshes."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:392
msgid ""
"Deleting nodes that are not used in the game engine (like specific lights "
"used for modelling)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:394
msgid ""
"To simplify this workflow, Rebel Editor offers several suffixes that can be "
"added to the names of the objects in your 3D modelling software. When "
"imported, Rebel Editor will detect suffixes in object names and will perform "
"actions automatically."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:400
msgid "All the suffixes described below are *case-sensitive*."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:403
msgid "Remove nodes (-noimp)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:405
msgid ""
"Objects that have the ``-noimp`` suffix will be removed at import-time no "
"matter what their type is. They will not appear in the imported scene."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:409
msgid "Create collisions (-col, -convcol, -colonly, -convcolonly)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:411
msgid ""
"The option ``-col`` will work only for Mesh objects. If it is detected, a "
"child static collision node will be added, using the same geometry as the "
"mesh. This will create a triangle mesh collision shape, which is a slow, but "
"accurate option for collision detection. This option is usually what you "
"want for level geometry (but see also ``-colonly`` below)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:417
msgid ""
"The option ``-convcol`` will create a :ref:`class_convexpolygonshape` "
"instead of a :ref:`class_concavepolygonshape`. Unlike triangle meshes which "
"can be concave, a convex shape can only accurately represent a shape that "
"doesn't have any concave angles (a pyramid is convex, but a hollow box is "
"concave). Due to this, convex collision shapes are generally not suited for "
"level geometry. When representing simple enough meshes, convex collision "
"shapes can result in better performance compared to a triangle collision "
"shape. This option is ideal for simple or dynamic objects that require "
"mostly-accurate collision detection."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:426
msgid ""
"However, in both cases, the visual geometry may be too complex or not smooth "
"enough for collisions. This can create physics glitches and slow down the "
"engine unnecessarily."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:430
msgid ""
"To solve this, the ``-colonly`` modifier exists. It will remove the mesh "
"upon importing and will create a :ref:`class_staticbody` collision instead. "
"This helps the visual mesh and actual collision to be separated."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:434
msgid ""
"The option ``-convcolonly`` works in a similar way, but will create a :ref:"
"`class_convexpolygonshape` instead."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:436
msgid ""
"The option ``-colonly`` can also be used with Blender's empty objects. On "
"import, it will create a :ref:`class_staticbody` with a collision node as a "
"child. The collision node will have one of a number of predefined shapes, "
"depending on Blender's empty draw type:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:443
msgid "Single arrow will create a :ref:`class_rayshape`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:444
msgid "Cube will create a :ref:`class_boxshape`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:445
msgid "Image will create a :ref:`class_planeshape`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:446
msgid ""
"Sphere (and the others not listed) will create a :ref:`class_sphereshape`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:448
msgid ""
"When possible, **try to use a few primitive collision shapes** instead of "
"triangle mesh or convex shapes. Primitive shapes often have the best "
"performance and reliability."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:454
msgid ""
"For better visibility in Blender's editor, you can set the \"X-Ray\" option "
"on collision empties and set some distinct color for them in Blender's "
"**User Preferences > Themes > 3D View > Empty**."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:460
msgid ""
"See :doc:`/tutorials/physics/collision_shapes_3d` for a comprehensive "
"overview of collision shapes."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:464
msgid "Create navigation (-navmesh)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:466
msgid ""
"A mesh node with the ``-navmesh`` suffix will be converted to a navigation "
"mesh. The original Mesh object will be removed at import-time."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:470
msgid "Create a VehicleBody (-vehicle)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:472
msgid ""
"A mesh node with the ``-vehicle`` suffix will be imported as a child to a :"
"ref:`class_VehicleBody` node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:476
msgid "Create a VehicleWheel (-wheel)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:478
msgid ""
"A mesh node with the ``-wheel`` suffix will be imported as a child to a :ref:"
"`class_VehicleWheel` node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:482
msgid "Rigid Body (-rigid)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:484
msgid ""
"A mesh node with the ``-rigid`` suffix will be imported as a :ref:"
"`class_RigidBody`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:487
msgid "Animation loop (-loop, -cycle)"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:489
msgid ""
"Animation clips in the COLLADA document that start or end with the token "
"``loop`` or ``cycle`` will be imported as a Rebel Animation with the loop "
"flag set. **Unlike the other suffixes described above, this does not require "
"a hyphen.**"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.rst:493
msgid ""
"In Blender, this requires using the NLA Editor and naming the Action with "
"the ``loop`` or ``cycle`` prefix or suffix."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:2
msgid "Importing translations"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:5
msgid "Games and internationalization"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:7
msgid ""
"The world is full of different languages and cultures, so nowadays games are "
"released in several languages. To handle this, internationalized text must "
"be supported in any modern game engine."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:11
msgid ""
"In regular desktop or mobile applications, internationalized text is usually "
"located in resource files (or .po files for GNU stuff). Games, however, can "
"use several orders of magnitude more text than applications, so they must "
"support efficient methods for dealing with loads of multilingual text."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:17
msgid ""
"There are two approaches to generate multilingual language games and "
"applications. Both are based on a key:value system. The first is to use one "
"of the languages as the key (usually English), the second is to use a "
"specific identifier. The first approach is probably easier for development "
"if a game is released first in English, later in other languages, but a "
"complete nightmare if working with many languages at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:25
msgid ""
"In general, games use the second approach and a unique ID is used for each "
"string. This allows you to revise the text while it is being translated to "
"other languages. The unique ID can be a number, a string, or a string with a "
"number (it's just a unique string anyway)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:30
msgid ""
"If you need a more powerful file format, Rebel Editor also supports loading "
"translations written in the gettext ``.po`` format. See :doc:`/tutorials/"
"i18n/localization_using_gettext` for details."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:35
msgid "Translation format"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:37
msgid ""
"To complete the picture and allow efficient support for translations, Rebel "
"Editor has a special importer that can read CSV files. Most spreadsheet "
"editors can export to this format, so the only requirement is that the files "
"have a special arrangement. The CSV files **must** be saved with UTF-8 "
"encoding without a `byte order mark <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Byte_order_mark>`__."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:45
msgid ""
"By default, Microsoft Excel will always save CSV files with ANSI encoding "
"rather than UTF-8. There is no built-in way to do this, but there are "
"workarounds as described `here <https://stackoverflow.com/questions/4221176/"
"excel-to-csv-with-utf8-encoding>`__."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:50
msgid ""
"We recommend using `LibreOffice <https://www.libreoffice.org/>`__ or Google "
"Sheets instead."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:52
msgid "CSV files must be formatted as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:55
#: tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_translations.rst:74
