msgid "Tips"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:66
msgid ""
"Sometimes it can be useful to use the ``using static`` directive. This "
"directive allows to access the members and nested types of a class without "
"specifying the class name."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:84
msgid "Export keyword"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:86
msgid ""
"Use the ``[Export]`` attribute instead of the GDScript ``export`` keyword. "
"This attribute can also be provided with optional :ref:"
"`PropertyHint<enum_@GlobalScope_PropertyHint>` and ``hintString`` "
"parameters. Default values can be set by assigning a value."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:112
msgid "Signal keyword"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:114
msgid ""
"Use the ``[Signal]`` attribute to declare a signal instead of the GDScript "
"``signal`` keyword. This attribute should be used on a `delegate`, whose "
"name signature will be used to define the signal."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:122
msgid ""
"See also: :ref:`tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_features:c# signals`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:125
msgid "`onready` keyword"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:127
msgid ""
"GDScript has the ability to defer the initialization of a member variable "
"until the ready function is called with `onready` (cf. :ref:`tutorials/"
"scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics:onready keyword`). For example:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:135
msgid ""
"However C# does not have this ability. To achieve the same effect you need "
"to do this."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:147
msgid "Singletons"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:149
msgid ""
"Singletons are available as static classes rather than using the singleton "
"pattern. This is to make code less verbose than it would be with an "
"``Instance`` property."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:158
msgid ""
"However, in some very rare cases this is not enough. For example, you may "
"want to access a member from the base class ``Rebel.Object``, like "
"``Connect``. For such use cases we provide a static property named "
"``Singleton`` that returns the singleton instance. The type of this instance "
"is ``Rebel.Object``."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:172
msgid ""
"Use ``System.String`` (``string``). Most of Rebel Engine's String methods "
"are provided by the ``StringExtensions`` class as extension methods."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:182
msgid "There are a few differences, though:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:184
msgid ""
"``erase``: Strings are immutable in C#, so we cannot modify the string "
"passed to the extension method. For this reason, ``Erase`` was added as an "
"extension method of ``StringBuilder`` instead of string. Alternatively, you "
"can use ``string.Remove``."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:188
msgid ""
"``IsSubsequenceOf``/``IsSubsequenceOfi``: An additional method is provided, "
"which is an overload of ``IsSubsequenceOf``, allowing you to explicitly "
"specify case sensitivity:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/c_sharp/c_sharp_differences.rst:199
msgid ""
"``Match``/``Matchn``/``ExprMatch``: An additional method is provided besides "
"``Match`` and ``Matchn``, which allows you to explicitly specify case "
"sensitivity:"
msgstr ""

