#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:160
msgid ""
"In the next block we're defining our methods, we obviously have our "
"constructor and destructor defined, but there are two other functions that "
"will likely look familiar to some, and one new method."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:164
msgid ""
"The first is ``_register_methods``, which is a static function that Rebel "
"Engine will call to find out which methods can be called on our NativeScript "
"and which properties it exposes. The second is our ``_process`` function, "
"which will work exactly the same as the ``_process`` function you're used to "
"in GDScript. The third is our ``_init`` function which is called after Rebel "
"Engine has properly set up our object. It has to exist even if you don't "
"place any code in it."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:171
msgid "Let's implement our functions by creating our ``gdexample.cpp`` file:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:203
msgid ""
"This one should be straightforward. We're implementing each method of our "
"class that we defined in our header file. Note that the ``register_method`` "
"call **must** expose the ``_process`` method, otherwise Rebel Engine will "
"not be able to use it. However, we do not have to tell Rebel Engine about "
"our constructor, destructor and ``_init`` functions."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:209
msgid ""
"The other method of note is our ``_process`` function, which simply keeps "
"track of how much time has passed and calculates a new position for our "
"sprite using a sine and cosine function. What stands out is calling ``owner-"
">set_position`` to call one of the built-in methods of our Sprite. This is "
"because our class is a container class; ``owner`` points to the actual "
"Sprite node our script relates to."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:216
msgid ""
"There is one more C++ file we need; we'll name it ``gdlibrary.cpp``. Our "
"GDNative plugin can contain multiple NativeScripts, each with their own "
"header and source file like we've implemented ``GDExample`` up above. What "
"we need now is a small bit of code that tells Rebel Engine about all the "
"NativeScripts in our GDNative plugin."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:240
msgid ""
"Note that we are not using the ``godot`` namespace here, since the three "
"functions implemented here need to be defined without a namespace."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:243
msgid ""
"The ``godot_gdnative_init`` and ``godot_gdnative_terminate`` functions get "
"called respectively when Rebel Engine loads our plugin and when it unloads "
"it. All we're doing here is parse through the functions in our bindings "
"module to initialize them, but you might have to set up more things "
"depending on your needs."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example.rst:249
msgid ""
"The important function is the third function called "
"``godot_nativescript_init``. We first call a function in our bindings "
"library that does its usual stuff. After that, we call the function "
"``register_class`` for each of our classes in our library."
msgstr ""

