#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:2
msgid "What is GDNative?"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:7
msgid ""
"**GDNative** lets the engine interact with native `shared libraries <https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_(computing)#Shared_libraries>`__ at run-time. "
"You can use it to run native code without compiling it with the engine."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:11
msgid ""
"GDNative is *not* a scripting language and has no relation to :doc:`GDScript "
"</tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics>`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:15
msgid "Differences between GDNative and C++ modules"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:17
msgid ""
"You can use both GDNative and :doc:`C++ modules </development/engine/"
"custom_modules_in_cpp>` to run C or C++ code in a Rebel Project."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:20
msgid ""
"They also both allow you to integrate third-party libraries into Rebel "
"Engine. The one you should choose depends on your needs."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:24
msgid "Advantages of GDNative"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:26
msgid ""
"Unlike modules, GDNative doesn't require compiling the engine's source code, "
"making it easier to distribute your work. It gives you access to most of the "
"API available to GDScript C#, allowing you to code game logic with full "
"control regarding performance. It's ideal if you need high-performance code "
"you'd like to distribute as an add-on."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:32
msgid "Also:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:34
msgid ""
"GDNative is not limited to C and C++. Thanks to :ref:`third-party bindings "
"<tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative:version compatibility>`, you "
"can use it with many other languages."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:37
msgid ""
"You can use the same compiled GDNative library in the editor and exported "
"project. With C++ modules, you have to recompile all the export templates "
"you plan to use if you require its functionality at run-time."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:40
msgid ""
"GDNative only requires you to compile your library, not the whole engine. "
"That's unlike C++ modules, which are statically compiled into the engine. "
"Every time you change a module, you need to recompile the engine. Even with "
"incremental builds, this process is slower than using GDNative."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:46
msgid "Advantages of C++ modules"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:48
msgid ""
"We recommend :doc:`C++ modules </development/engine/custom_modules_in_cpp>` "
"in cases where GDNative isn't enough:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:51
msgid ""
"C++ modules provide deeper integration into the engine. GDNative's access is "
"limited to what the scripting API exposes."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:53
msgid ""
"You can use C++ modules to provide additional features in a project without "
"carrying native library files around. This extends to exported projects."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:55
msgid ""
"C++ modules are supported on all platforms. In contrast, GDNative has only "
"limited support on HTML5 (cannot be used together with multi-threading), and "
"is not supported on Universal Windows Platform (UWP)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:58
msgid ""
"C++ modules can be faster than GDNative, especially when the code requires a "
"lot of communication through the scripting API."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:62
msgid "Supported languages"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:64
msgid ""
"The Rebel developers officially support the following language bindings for "
"GDNative:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:67
msgid ""
"C++ :doc:`(tutorial) </tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_cpp_example>`"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:68
msgid "C :doc:`(tutorial) </tutorials/scripting/gdnative/gdnative_c_example>`"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:72
msgid ""
"There are no plans to support additional languages with GDNative officially. "
"That said, the community offers several bindings for other languages (see "
"below)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:77
msgid "Version compatibility"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:79
msgid ""
":doc:`Unlike Rebel Engine </about/release_policy>`, GDNative has stricter "
"version compatibility requirements as it relies on low-level *ptrcalls* to "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdnative/what_is_gdnative.rst:82
msgid ""
"GDNative add-ons compiled for a given Rebel Engine version are only "
"guaranteed to work with the same minor release series."
msgstr ""

