msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:142
msgid ""
"Type casting is a key concept in typed languages. Casting is the conversion "
"of a value from one type to another."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:145
msgid ""
"Imagine an Enemy in your game, that ``extends Area2D``. You want it to "
"collide with the Player, a ``KinematicBody2D`` with a script called "
"``PlayerController`` attached to it. You use the ``on_body_entered`` signal "
"to detect the collision. With typed code, the body you detect is going to be "
"a generic ``PhysicsBody2D``, and not your ``PlayerController`` on the "
"``_on_body_entered`` callback."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:152
msgid ""
"You can check if this ``PhysicsBody2D`` is your Player with the ``as`` "
"casting keyword, and using the colon ``:`` again to force the variable to "
"use this type. This forces the variable to stick to the ``PlayerController`` "
"type:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:166
msgid ""
"As we're dealing with a custom type, if the ``body`` doesn't extend "
"``PlayerController``, the ``player``\\ variable will be set to ``null``. We "
"can use this to check if the body is the player or not. We will also get "
"full autocompletion on the player variable thanks to that cast."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:173
msgid ""
"If you try to cast with a built-in type and it fails, Rebel Engine will "
"throw an error."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:176
msgid "Safe lines"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:178
msgid ""
"You can also use casting to ensure safe lines. Safe lines tell you when "
"ambiguous lines of code are type-safe. As you can mix and match typed and "
"dynamic code, at times, Rebel Editor doesn't have enough information to know "
"if an instruction will trigger an error or not at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:183
msgid ""
"This happens when you get a child node. Let's take a timer for example: with "
"dynamic code, you can get the node with ``$Timer``. GDScript supports `duck-"
"typing <https://stackoverflow.com/a/4205163/8125343>`__, so even if your "
"timer is of type ``Timer``, it is also a ``Node`` and an ``Object``, two "
"classes it extends. With dynamic GDScript, you also don't care about the "
"node's type as long as it has the methods you need to call."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/gdscript/static_typing.rst:191
msgid ""
"You can use casting to tell Rebel Engine the type you expect when you get a "
"node: ``($Timer as Timer)``, ``($Player as KinematicBody2D)``, etc. Rebel "
"Editor will ensure the type works and if so, the line number will turn green "
"at the left of the script editor."
msgstr ""

