#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:162
msgid ""
"After the engine freed the node, the remote scene tree doesn't display the "
"sprite anymore."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:167
msgid ""
"You can alternatively call ``free()`` to immediately destroy the node. You "
"should do this with care as any reference to it will instantly become "
"``null``. We recommend using ``queue_free()`` unless you know what you're "
"doing."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:171
msgid ""
"When you free a node, it also frees all its children. Thanks to this, to "
"delete an entire branch of the scene tree, you only have to free the topmost "
"parent node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:178
msgid ""
"Scenes are templates from which you can create as many reproductions as "
"you'd like. This operation is called instancing, and doing it from code "
"happens in two steps:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:182
msgid "Loading the scene from the hard drive."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:183
msgid ""
"Creating an instance of the loaded :ref:`PackedScene <class_PackedScene>` "
"resource."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:195
msgid ""
"Preloading the scene can improve the user's experience as the load operation "
"happens when the compiler reads the script and not at runtime. This feature "
"is only available with GDScript."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:204
msgid ""
"At that point, ``scene`` is a packed scene resource, not a node. To create "
"the actual node, you need to call :ref:`PackedScene.instance() "
"<class_PackedScene_method_instance>`. It returns a tree of nodes that you "
"can as a child of your current node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances.rst:220
msgid ""
"The advantage of this two-step process is you can keep a packed scene loaded "
"and create new instances on the fly. For example, to quickly instance "
"several enemies or bullets."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:2
msgid "Overridable functions"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:4
msgid ""
"Rebel Engine's Node class provides virtual functions you can override to "
"update nodes every frame or on specific events, like when they enter the "
"scene tree."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:7
msgid "This document presents the ones you'll use most often."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:9
msgid ""
"Under the hood, these functions rely on Rebel Engine's low-level "
"notifications system. To learn more about it, see :doc:`/tutorials/"
"best_practices/notifications`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:13
msgid ""
"Two functions allow you to initialize and get nodes, besides the class's "
"constructor: ``_enter_tree()`` and ``_ready()``."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:16
msgid ""
"When the node enters the Scene Tree, it becomes active and the engine calls "
"its ``_enter_tree()`` method. That node's children may not be part of the "
"active scene yet. As you can remove and re-add nodes to the scene tree, this "
"function may be called multiple times throughout a node's lifetime."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:21
msgid ""
"Most of the time, you'll use ``_ready()`` instead. This function is called "
"only once in a node's lifetime, after ``_enter_tree()``. ``_ready()`` "
"ensures that all children have entered the scene tree first, so you can "
"safely call ``get_node()`` on it."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:25
msgid ""
"To learn more about getting node references, read :doc:"
"`nodes_and_scene_instances`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:28
msgid ""
"Another related callback is ``_exit_tree()``, which the engine calls every "
"time a node exits the scene tree. This can be when you call :ref:`Node."
"remove_child() <class_Node_method_remove_child>` or when you free a node."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:69
msgid ""
"The two virtual methods ``_process()`` and ``_physics_process()`` allow you "
"to update the node, every frame and every physics frame respectively. For "
"more information, read the dedicated documentation: :doc:"
"`idle_and_physics_processing`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:99
msgid ""
"Two more essential built-in node callback functions are :ref:`Node."
"_unhandled_input() <class_Node_method__unhandled_input>` and :ref:`Node."
"_input() <class_Node_method__input>`, which you use to both receive and "
"process individual input events. The ``_unhandled_input()`` method receives "
"every key press, mouse click, etc. that have not been handled already in an "
"``_input()`` callback or in a user interface component. You want to use it "
"for gameplay input in general. The ``_input()`` callback allows you to "
"intercept and process input events before ``_unhandled_input()`` gets them."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:108
msgid ""
"To learn more about inputs in Rebel Engine, see the :ref:`Input section <toc-"
"learn-features-inputs>`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/overridable_functions.rst:137
msgid ""
"There are some more overridable functions like :ref:`Node."
"_get_configuration_warning() "
"<class_Node_method__get_configuration_warning>`. Specialized node types "
"provide more callbacks like :ref:`CanvasItem._draw() "
"<class_CanvasItem_method__draw>` to draw programmatically or :ref:`Control."
"_gui_input() <class_Control_method__gui_input>` to handle clicks and input "
"on UI elements."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:2
msgid "Pausing games"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:5
msgid "Pause?"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:7
msgid ""
"In most games it is desirable to, at some point, interrupt the game to do "
"something else, such as taking a break or changing options. However, this is "
"not as simple as it seems. The game might be stopped, but it might be "
"desirable that some menus and animations continue working."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:13
msgid ""
"Implementing a fine-grained control for what can be paused (and what can "
"not) is a lot of work, so a simple framework for pausing is provided in "
"Rebel Engine."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:18
msgid "How pausing works"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:20
msgid ""
"To set pause mode, the pause state must be set. This is done by assigning "
"``true`` to the :ref:`SceneTree.paused <class_SceneTree_property_paused>` "
"property:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:32
msgid "Doing so will have the following behavior:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:34
msgid "2D and 3D physics will be stopped."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:35
msgid ""
"``_process`` and ``_physics_process`` will not be called anymore in nodes."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:36
msgid "``_input`` and ``_input_event`` will not be called anymore either."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:38
msgid ""
"This effectively stops the whole game. Calling this function from a script, "
"by default, will result in an unrecoverable state (nothing will work "
"anymore!)."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:43
msgid "White-listing nodes"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:45
msgid ""
"Before enabling pause, make sure that nodes that must keep working during "
"pause are white-listed. This is done by editing the \"Pause Mode\" property "
"in a node:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:51
msgid "You can achieve the same result in code:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:67
msgid ""
"By default, all nodes have this property in the \"Inherit\" state. This "
"means, that they will only process (or not) depending on what this same "
"property is set on the parent node. If the parent is set to \"Inherit\" , "
"then the grandparent will be checked and so on. Ultimately, if a state can't "
"be found in any of the grandparents, the pause state in SceneTree is used. "
"This means that, by default, when the game is paused every node will be "
"paused."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:75
msgid "So the three possible states for a node are:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:77
msgid ""
"**Inherit**: Process depending on the state of the parent, grandparent, etc. "
"The first parent that has a non-Inherit state."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:79
msgid ""
"**Stop**: Stop the node no matter what (and children in Inherit mode). When "
"paused this node will not process."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:81
msgid ""
"**Process**: Process the node no matter what (and children in Inherit mode). "
"Paused or not this node will process."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:87
msgid ""
"An example of this is creating a popup or panel with controls inside, and "
"set its pause mode to \"Process\" then hide it:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:92
msgid ""
"Just by setting the root of the pause popup to \"Process\", all children and "
"grandchildren will inherit that state. This way, this branch of the scene "
"tree will continue working when paused."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:96
msgid ""
"Finally, make it so when a pause button is pressed (any button will do), "
"enable the pause and show the pause screen."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/scripting/pausing_games.rst:114
msgid "To remove the pause, do the opposite when the pause screen is closed:"
msgstr ""

