#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:2
msgid "Introduction to shaders"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:4
msgid ""
"This page explains what shaders are and will give you an overview of how "
"they work in Rebel Engine. For a detailed reference of the Rebel Shading "
"Language, see :doc:`/tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/shading_language`."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:8
msgid ""
"Shaders are a special kind of program that runs on Graphics Processing Units "
"(GPUs). They were initially used to shade 3D scenes but can nowadays do much "
"more. You can use them to control how the engine draws geometry and pixels "
"on the screen, allowing you to achieve all sorts of effects."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:13
msgid ""
"Modern rendering engines like Rebel Engine draw everything with shaders: "
"graphics cards can run thousands of instructions in parallel, leading to "
"incredible rendering speed."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:17
msgid ""
"Because of their parallel nature, though, shaders don't process information "
"the way a typical program does. Shader code runs on each vertex or pixel in "
"isolation. You cannot store data between frames either. As a result, when "
"working with shaders, you need to code and think differently from other "
"programming languages."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:23
msgid ""
"Suppose you want to update all the pixels in a texture to a given color. In "
"GDScript, your code would use ``for`` loops::"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:30
msgid ""
"Your code is already part of a loop in a shader, so the corresponding code "
"would look like this."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:41
msgid ""
"The graphics card calls the ``fragment()`` function once or more for each "
"pixel it has to draw. More on that below."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:44
msgid "Shaders in Rebel Engine"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:46
msgid ""
"Rebel Shading Language is based on the popular OpenGL Shading Language "
"(GLSL) but simplified. The engine handles some of the lower-level "
"initialization work for you, making it easier to write complex shaders."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:50
msgid ""
"In Rebel Shading Language, shaders are made up of three main functions: "
"``vertex()``, ``fragment()``, and ``light()``."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:53
msgid ""
"The ``vertex()`` function runs over all the vertices in the mesh and sets "
"their positions and some other per-vertex variables."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:56
msgid ""
"The ``fragment()`` function runs for every pixel covered by the mesh. It "
"uses values output by the ``vertex()`` function, interpolated between the "
"vertices."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:60
msgid ""
"The ``light()`` function runs for every pixel and for every light. It takes "
"variables from the ``fragment()`` function and from its previous runs."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:65
msgid ""
"The ``light()`` function won't run if the ``vertex_lighting`` render mode is "
"enabled, or if **Rendering > Quality > Shading > Force Vertex Shading** is "
"enabled in the Project Settings. It's enabled by default on mobile platforms."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:71
msgid "Shader types"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:73
msgid ""
"Instead of supplying a general-purpose configuration for all uses (2D, 3D, "
"particles), you must specify the type of shader you're writing. Different "
"types support different render modes, built-in variables, and processing "
"functions."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:77
msgid ""
"In Rebel Shading Language, all shaders need to specify their type in the "
"first line, like so:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:83
msgid "Here are the available types:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:85
msgid ""
":doc:`spatial </tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/spatial_shader>` for 3D "
"rendering."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:86
msgid ""
":doc:`canvas_item </tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/canvas_item_shader>` "
"for 2D rendering."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:87
msgid ""
":doc:`particles </tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/particle_shader>` for "
"particle systems."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:90
#: tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/canvas_item_shader.rst:12
#: tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/particle_shader.rst:25
#: tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/spatial_shader.rst:10
msgid "Render modes"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:92
msgid ""
"Shaders have optional render modes you can specify on the second line, after "
"the shader type, like so:"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:100
msgid ""
"Render modes alter the way Rebel Engine applies the shader. For example, the "
"``unshaded`` mode makes the engine skip the built-in light processor "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:103
msgid ""
"Each shader type has different render modes. See the reference for each "
"shader type for a complete list of render modes."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:107
msgid "Processor functions"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:109
msgid ""
"Depending on the shader type, you can override different processor "
"functions. For ``spatial`` and ``canvas_item``, you have access to "
"``vertex()``, ``fragment()``, and ``light()``. For ``particles``, you only "
"have access to ``vertex()``."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:115
msgid "Vertex processor"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:117
msgid ""
"The ``vertex()`` processing function is called once for every vertex in "
"``spatial`` and ``canvas_item`` shaders. For ``particles`` shaders, it is "
"called once for every particle."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:121
msgid ""
"Each vertex in your world's geometry has properties like a position and "
"color. The function modifies those values and passes them to the fragment "
"function. You can also use it to send extra data to the fragment function "
"using varyings."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:125
msgid ""
"By default, Rebel Engine transforms your vertex information for you, which "
"is necessary to project geometry onto the screen. You can use render modes "
"to transform the data yourself; see the :doc:`Spatial shader doc </tutorials/"
"shaders/shader_reference/spatial_shader>` for an example."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:131
msgid "Fragment processor"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:133
msgid ""
"The ``fragment()`` processing function is used to set up the Rebel Material "
"parameters per pixel. This code runs on every visible pixel the object or "
"primitive draws. It is only available in ``spatial`` and ``canvas_item`` "
"shaders."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:137
msgid ""
"The standard use of the fragment function is to set up material properties "
"used to calculate lighting. For example, you would set values for "
"``ROUGHNESS``, ``RIM``, or ``TRANSMISSION``, which would tell the light "
"function how the lights respond to that fragment. This makes it possible to "
"control a complex shading pipeline without the user having to write much "
"code. If you don't need this built-in functionality, you can ignore it and "
"write your own light processing function, and Rebel Engine will optimize it "
"away. For example, if you do not write a value to ``RIM``, Rebel Engine will "
"not calculate rim lighting. During compilation, Rebel Engine checks to see "
"if ``RIM`` is used; if not, it cuts all the corresponding code out. "
"Therefore, you will not waste calculations on the effects that you do not "
"use."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:150
msgid "Light processor"
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:152
msgid ""
"The ``light()`` processor runs per pixel too, and it runs once for every "
"light that affects the object. It does not run if no lights affect the "
"object. It exists as a function called inside the ``fragment()`` processor "
"and typically operates on the material properties setup inside the "
"``fragment()`` function."
msgstr ""

#: tutorials/shaders/introduction_to_shaders.rst:157
msgid ""
"The ``light()`` processor works differently in 2D than it does in 3D; for a "
"description of how it works in each, see their documentation, :doc:"
"`CanvasItem shaders </tutorials/shaders/shader_reference/"
"canvas_item_shader>` and :doc:`Spatial shaders </tutorials/shaders/"
"shader_reference/spatial_shader>`, respectively."
msgstr ""

